    "heading": "13. Review questions and exercises",
    "body": "## Conceptual questions\n\n1. What is a qubit?\n2. What do the labels \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\) represent?\n3. Why are \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\) called basis states?\n4. What is Dirac notation?\n5. What is the difference between a ket and a bra?\n6. Why must a qubit state be normalized?\n7. What is the difference between an amplitude and a probability?\n8. What happens to a qubit after a computational-basis measurement?\n9. Explain the difference between a pure superposition and a classical mixture.\n10. Why can a relative phase matter even when it does not change immediate computational-basis probabilities?\n\n## Numerical exercises\n\n1. Verify that\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{3}{5}|0\\rangle+\\frac{4}{5}|1\\rangle\n   \\]\n   is normalized.\n\n2. Calculate the measurement probabilities for\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}|0\\rangle+\\sqrt{\\frac23}|1\\rangle.\n   \\]\n\n3. Determine whether\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{2}|0\\rangle+\\frac{1}{2}|1\\rangle\n   \\]\n   is normalized. If not, normalize it.\n\n4. Find \\(\\beta\\) if\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle\n   \\]\n   is normalized.\n\n5. Calculate \\(\\langle0|\\psi\\rangle\\) and \\(\\langle1|\\psi\\rangle\\) for\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}|0\\rangle-\\frac{i}{\\sqrt2}|1\\rangle.\n   \\]\n\n6. Apply the Pauli-X gate to\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}|0\\rangle+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}|1\\rangle.\n   \\]\n\n7. Apply the Hadamard gate to \\(|0\\rangle\\) and calculate the measurement probabilities.\n\n8. Apply \\(H\\), then \\(Z\\), then \\(H\\) to \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\n## Programming activity\n\nUsing a quantum programming framework, create a one-qubit circuit that:\n\n1. Initializes the qubit in \\(|0\\rangle\\).\n2. Measures it 100 times and records the result.\n3. Applies an X gate and measures again.\n4. Applies an H gate and measures 1,000 times.\n5. Compares the observed frequencies with the theoretical probabilities.\n6. Applies \\(H\\)-\\(Z\\)-\\(H\\) and verify the final state by measurement.\n\n---"
