  textbookContent: "# Chapter 3: Superposition\n\n## 3.1 Chapter overview\n\n**Superposition** is one of the central ideas of quantum computing. It explains how a qubit can be described as a linear combination of the computational basis states \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\). A general qubit state is written as\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are probability amplitudes. The amplitudes may be real or complex and must satisfy the normalization condition\n\n\\[\n|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n\\]\n\nWhen the qubit is measured in the computational basis, the result is 0 or 1. The probabilities are determined by the squared magnitudes of the amplitudes:\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\nSuperposition must be distinguished from ordinary classical uncertainty. A classical uncertain system may be in one definite state even when an observer does not know which state it is. A quantum superposition is a physical state with amplitudes and relative phase. These amplitudes can interfere under later quantum operations.\n\n---\n\n## 3.2 Learning objectives\n\nAfter studying this chapter, learners should be able to:\n\n1. Define quantum superposition.\n2. Construct superpositions of \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n3. Explain the meaning of probability amplitudes.\n4. Calculate measurement probabilities.\n5. Normalize a proposed quantum state.\n6. Distinguish a quantum superposition from a classical probability distribution.\n7. Explain the role of relative phase.\n8. Demonstrate how a Hadamard gate creates a superposition.\n9. Explain how later gates can reveal phase through interference.\n10. Solve numerical and conceptual problems involving superposition.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Meaning of superposition\n\n## 4.1 Linear combination of basis states\n\nThe computational basis states of a qubit are\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nA general pure qubit state is a linear combination of these states:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nWhen both coefficients are nonzero, the qubit is said to be in a **superposition** of \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\nThe word “linear” is important. The basis states are multiplied by coefficients and then added as vectors. This is not the same as saying that the qubit is an ordinary classical object that simultaneously possesses two definite values.\n\nThe coefficients contain information about the state. Their squared magnitudes determine the probabilities of measurement outcomes, while their relative phase affects future interference.\n\n## 4.2 Examples of superposition states\n\nThe equal superposition states are\n\n\\[\n|+\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n|-\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nBoth are normalized because\n\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\right|^2+\n\\left|\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\right|^2\n=\\frac12+\\frac12=1.\n\\]\n\nThey have equal computational-basis measurement probabilities, but they have different relative phases. That difference becomes observable when another quantum operation is applied.\n\nAnother valid superposition is\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}|0\\rangle+\nrac{2}{\\sqrt5}|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThis state gives unequal probabilities because the amplitudes have different magnitudes.\n\n## 4.3 What superposition does not mean\n\nSuperposition does not mean that a measurement produces both 0 and 1 in one ordinary result. A measurement in the computational basis produces one outcome. If the state is\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\]\n\nthen one measurement gives either 0 or 1. Repeated preparations and measurements reveal the probability distribution.\n\nSuperposition also does not mean that a quantum computer automatically evaluates all possible answers and makes all of them available for reading. The computer must use quantum operations to create useful interference before measurement.\n\n## 4.4 Superposition as a state of amplitudes\n\nA quantum state contains amplitudes rather than directly accessible classical probabilities. For example,\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\]\n\nhas amplitudes\n\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\frac1{\\sqrt2},\n\\qquad\n\\beta=-\\frac1{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThe measurement probabilities are still\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nThe negative sign does not change these immediate probabilities, but it changes the way amplitudes combine during subsequent operations. Therefore, the state is different from\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n# 5. Superposition of \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\)\n\n## 5.1 General form\n\nThe most general pure one-qubit superposition is\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nwith\n\n\\[\n\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb{C}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n\\]\n\nThe symbols \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are not required to be positive. They may be negative or complex. However, their squared magnitudes must produce valid probabilities.\n\n## 5.2 Creating superposition with the Hadamard gate\n\nThe Hadamard gate is\n\n\\[\nH=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n1&1\\\\\n1&-1\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nIts action on the basis states is\n\n\\[\nH|0\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nH|1\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, applying \\(H\\) to a basis state produces an equal superposition.\n\nStarting from \\(|0\\rangle\\),\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle\n\\xrightarrow{H}\n|+\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nStarting from \\(|1\\rangle\\),\n\n\\[\n|1\\rangle\n\\xrightarrow{H}\n|-\\rangle.\n\\]\n\n## 5.3 Matrix derivation\n\nUsing column-vector notation,\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nApplying \\(H\\),\n\n\\[\nH|0\\rangle\n=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}\n=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nH|0\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\[\nH|1\\rangle\n=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}\n=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix},\n\\]\n\nso\n\n\\[\nH|1\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\n## 5.4 Unequal superposition\n\nSuperposition does not require equal amplitudes. For example,\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}|0\\rangle+\\frac12|1\\rangle\n\\]\n\nis a valid superposition. Its probabilities are\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\left|\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right|^2=\\frac34,\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nP(1)=\\left|\\frac12\\right|^2=\\frac14.\n\\]\n\nThe state is still a superposition because both basis-state amplitudes are nonzero.\n\n## 5.5 Superposition with complex amplitudes\n\nConsider\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}|0\\rangle+\n\\frac{i}{\\sqrt2}|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe probabilities are\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nThe factor \\(i\\) represents a relative phase of \\(\\pi/2\\) between the two components. This phase may be invisible in an immediate computational-basis measurement, but it affects later operations.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Probability amplitudes\n\n## 6.1 Amplitudes versus probabilities\n\nA probability amplitude is a coefficient in a quantum state. For\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle,\n\\]\n\n\\(\\alpha\\) is the amplitude of \\(|0\\rangle\\), and \\(\\beta\\) is the amplitude of \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\nA probability is obtained by taking the squared magnitude:\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\nIf the amplitude is complex, its magnitude must be calculated using the complex conjugate:\n\n\\[\n|z|^2=z^*z.\n\\]\n\nFor\n\n\\[\nz=a+ib,\n\\]\n\nwe have\n\n\\[\n|z|^2=a^2+b^2.\n\\]\n\n## 6.2 Why amplitudes may be negative or complex\n\nProbabilities must be real and nonnegative. Amplitudes do not have those restrictions. A negative or complex amplitude is useful because amplitudes can add and cancel.\n\nFor example, the amplitudes\n\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n-\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\n\\]\n\nhave the same magnitude and therefore the same individual measurement probabilities. When they are combined with other amplitudes, however, their signs can produce constructive or destructive interference.\n\n## 6.3 Relative phase\n\nConsider\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_1\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_2\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThe first state has relative phase 0. The second has relative phase \\(\\pi\\). Both have the same computational-basis probabilities, but they are not the same state.\n\nA global phase multiplies the entire state:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi'\\rangle=e^{i\\gamma}|\\psi\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nA global phase does not change measurement predictions. A relative phase changes the phase between components and can affect later interference.\n\n## 6.4 Amplitude addition and interference\n\nSuppose two computational paths contribute amplitudes \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). The combined amplitude is\n\n\\[\na+b.\n\\]\n\nThe probability is\n\n\\[\n|a+b|^2.\n\\]\n\nExpanding,\n\n\\[\n|a+b|^2=|a|^2+|b|^2+a^*b+ab^*.\n\\]\n\nThe terms \\(a^*b\\) and \\(ab^*\\) are the interference terms. If they are positive, the paths interfere constructively. If they are negative, they interfere destructively.\n\nThis is one of the most important differences between quantum amplitudes and classical probabilities. Classical probabilities are added directly for mutually exclusive alternatives. Quantum amplitudes are added first, and probabilities are calculated afterward.\n\n## 6.5 Worked example: destructive interference\n\nStart from \\(|0\\rangle\\), apply \\(H\\), then apply \\(H\\) again:\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle\n\\xrightarrow{H}\n\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\xrightarrow{H}\n|0\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nUsing linearity,\n\n\\[\nH\\left(\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\\right)\n=\\frac{H|0\\rangle+H|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the Hadamard transformations:\n\n\\[\n=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\n\\left(\n\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n+\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\right).\n\\]\n\nThe \\(|1\\rangle\\) amplitudes cancel:\n\n\\[\n\\frac12|1\\rangle-\\frac12|1\\rangle=0.\n\\]\n\nThe \\(|0\\rangle\\) amplitudes add:\n\n\\[\n\\frac12|0\\rangle+\\frac12|0\\rangle=|0\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final state is \\(|0\\rangle\\) with probability 1.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Measurement probabilities\n\n## 7.1 The Born rule\n\nFor\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nmeasurement in the computational basis gives\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\nThese probabilities satisfy\n\n\\[\nP(0)+P(1)=1.\n\\]\n\nThe rule that probabilities are squared magnitudes of amplitudes is called the **Born rule**.\n\n## 7.2 Projection form of the rule\n\nThe projectors for the computational basis are\n\n\\[\nP_0=|0\\rangle\\langle0|,\n\\qquad\nP_1=|1\\rangle\\langle1|.\n\\]\n\nThe probability of outcome 0 is\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\langle\\psi|P_0|\\psi\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nand the probability of outcome 1 is\n\n\\[\nP(1)=\\langle\\psi|P_1|\\psi\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nSince\n\n\\[\n\\langle0|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha,\n\\qquad\n\\langle1|\\psi\\rangle=\\beta,\n\\]\n\nwe recover\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\n## 7.3 State after measurement\n\nIf the result is 0, the state becomes \\(|0\\rangle\\). If the result is 1, the state becomes \\(|1\\rangle\\). Therefore, after measuring a superposition, the original superposition is generally no longer present.\n\nFor example,\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\]\n\nproduces either 0 or 1. If the result is 0, a second measurement in the same basis gives 0 with probability 1. If the result is 1, a second measurement gives 1 with probability 1.\n\n## 7.4 Repeated measurements and frequency\n\nQuantum probabilities are estimated through repeated experiments. Suppose\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}|0\\rangle+\\frac12|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe theoretical probabilities are\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\frac34,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\frac14.\n\\]\n\nIf the state is prepared and measured 1,000 times, the expected counts are approximately\n\n\\[\n750 \\text{ results of 0},\n\\qquad\n250 \\text{ results of 1}.\n\\]\n\nThe actual counts fluctuate. More repetitions generally make the observed frequencies approach the theoretical probabilities.\n\n## 7.5 Measurement in a different basis\n\nMeasurement probabilities depend on the measurement basis. A state that is uncertain in the computational basis may be definite in another basis.\n\nFor example,\n\n\\[\n|+\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\]\n\nhas equal probabilities in the computational basis. However, it is one of the basis states of the Hadamard basis. Measuring in the \\(\\{|+\\rangle,|-\\rangle\\}\\) basis gives \\(|+\\rangle\\) with certainty.\n\nThis fact is important: “the qubit is in a superposition” is incomplete unless the basis is specified. Superposition is always defined relative to a chosen basis.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Superposition versus classical uncertainty\n\n## 8.1 Classical uncertainty\n\nSuppose a classical coin is placed under a cup. It is either heads or tails, but an observer does not know which. The observer may describe the situation using probabilities:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{heads})=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(\\text{tails})=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nThis is classical uncertainty. The coin has a definite physical condition in the model, even though the observer lacks information about it.\n\nA classical random bit can be represented by a probability distribution\n\n\\[\nP(0)=p,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=1-p.\n\\]\n\nThe probabilities are nonnegative real numbers and add to one.\n\n## 8.2 Quantum superposition\n\nA quantum state may be\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe state is not merely a statement that the observer does not know whether the qubit is in 0 or 1. It contains amplitudes, including relative phase. Quantum operations can cause these amplitudes to interfere.\n\nFor example, compare the pure superposition\n\n\\[\n|+\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\]\n\nwith the classical mixture\n\n\\[\n\\rho_{\\text{mix}}=\\frac12|0\\rangle\\langle0|+\\frac12|1\\rangle\\langle1|.\n\\]\n\nBoth produce equal computational-basis probabilities. Apply a Hadamard gate.\n\nFor the pure superposition,\n\n\\[\nH|+\\rangle=|0\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThus, measurement after the Hadamard gives 0 with certainty.\n\nFor the classical mixture, the two possible states transform separately:\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle\\xrightarrow{H}|+\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n|1\\rangle\\xrightarrow{H}|-\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(|+\\rangle\\) and \\(|-\\rangle\\) give equal computational-basis probabilities. Therefore, the mixture remains 0 and 1 with equal probabilities after the Hadamard when measured in the computational basis.\n\nThe two situations can be distinguished by a quantum operation. This proves that superposition is not the same as classical uncertainty.\n\n## 8.3 Density-matrix comparison\n\nThe density matrix of \\(|+\\rangle\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\rho_+=|+\\rangle\\langle+|\n=\\frac12\n\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\1&1\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThe density matrix of the equal mixture is\n\n\\[\n\\rho_{\\text{mix}}=\\frac12\n\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal entries are the same, so the computational-basis probabilities are the same. The off-diagonal entries differ:\n\n- The superposition has nonzero off-diagonal terms.\n- The classical mixture has zero off-diagonal terms.\n\nThe off-diagonal terms represent coherence. Their presence enables interference.\n\n## 8.4 Comparison table\n\n| Feature | Classical uncertainty | Quantum superposition |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mathematical object | Probability distribution | State vector or density matrix |\n| Components | Probabilities | Probability amplitudes |\n| Values | Nonnegative real numbers | Real or complex numbers |\n| Relative phase | No equivalent role | Can affect later outcomes |\n| Interference | No amplitude interference | Constructive and destructive interference |\n| Measurement | Reveals a pre-existing classical value in the model | Produces an outcome and generally changes the state |\n| Copying | Classical information can be copied | Unknown quantum states cannot be copied perfectly |\n| Example | Hidden coin toss | \\((|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\) |\n\n## 8.5 Why the distinction matters\n\nIf superposition were only classical uncertainty, every quantum experiment could be simulated by choosing a hidden value in advance. Experiments involving interference and entanglement show that this simple classical description is insufficient.\n\nFor quantum computing, the distinction is essential. Quantum algorithms manipulate amplitudes before measurement. A classical probability distribution cannot reproduce all the phase-sensitive interference patterns produced by quantum circuits without, in general, using additional computational resources.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Worked examples\n\n## Example 3.1: Finding measurement probabilities\n\nGiven\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}|0\\rangle+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}|1\\rangle,\n\\]\n\ncalculate the measurement probabilities.\n\nThe amplitudes are\n\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\frac1{\\sqrt5},\n\\qquad\n\\beta=\\frac2{\\sqrt5}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2=\\frac15,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2=\\frac45.\n\\]\n\nThe probabilities add to one:\n\n\\[\n\\frac15+\\frac45=1.\n\\]\n\n## Example 3.2: Normalizing a superposition\n\nConsider the unnormalized vector\n\n\\[\n|\\phi\\rangle=|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nIts norm squared is\n\n\\[\n\\langle\\phi|\\phi\\rangle=1+1=2.\n\\]\n\nThe normalization factor is\n\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThe normalized state is\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\n## Example 3.3: Complex amplitudes\n\nLet\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1+i}{2}|0\\rangle+\\frac{1-i}{2}|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nFor the first amplitude,\n\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{1+i}{2}\\right|^2\n=\\frac{1^2+1^2}{4}=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nFor the second amplitude,\n\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{1-i}{2}\\right|^2=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nP(0)=P(1)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\n## Example 3.4: Hadamard followed by measurement\n\nStart in \\(|0\\rangle\\) and apply \\(H\\):\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle\\xrightarrow{H}|+\\rangle\n=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThe measurement probabilities are\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\n## Example 3.5: Hadamard twice\n\nStart in \\(|0\\rangle\\), apply \\(H\\), and apply \\(H\\) again:\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle\\xrightarrow{H}|+\\rangle\\xrightarrow{H}|0\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe final outcome is 0 with probability 1. The second Hadamard causes destructive interference for the 1 outcome.\n\n## Example 3.6: Superposition versus mixture\n\nState A is the pure state\n\n\\[\n|+\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nState B is a classical mixture with 50 percent \\(|0\\rangle\\) and 50 percent \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\nBoth give equal computational-basis probabilities. After applying \\(H\\):\n\n\\[\nH|+\\rangle=|0\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nso State A gives 0 with certainty.\n\nFor State B, half of the systems transform to \\(|+\\rangle\\) and half transform to \\(|-\\rangle\\). Measuring in the computational basis gives equal numbers of 0 and 1 on average.\n\nThe states have identical initial computational-basis statistics but different behavior under a quantum operation.\n\n---\n\n# 10. Common misconceptions\n\n## Misconception 1: Superposition means two classical values can be read simultaneously\n\nA measurement gives one outcome. Superposition concerns the amplitudes used during quantum evolution, not the simultaneous display of multiple classical values.\n\n## Misconception 2: The coefficients \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are probabilities\n\nThey are amplitudes. The probabilities are \\(|\\alpha|^2\\) and \\(|\\beta|^2\\).\n\n## Misconception 3: Negative amplitudes are impossible\n\nAmplitudes may be negative or complex. Probabilities, not amplitudes, must be nonnegative.\n\n## Misconception 4: Equal measurement probabilities imply identical quantum states\n\nThe states \\(|+\\rangle\\) and \\(|-\\rangle\\) have the same computational-basis probabilities but different relative phases. They can produce different results after a later gate.\n\n## Misconception 5: Superposition is just lack of knowledge\n\nA quantum superposition contains coherence and can interfere. A classical mixture represents ordinary uncertainty and does not contain the same phase information.\n\n## Misconception 6: Superposition automatically gives a speedup\n\nAn algorithm must use interference to extract useful information. Superposition alone does not guarantee computational advantage.\n\n---\n\n# 11. Chapter summary\n\nSuperposition is the ability of a qubit to exist in a normalized linear combination of basis states:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe amplitudes satisfy\n\n\\[\n|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n\\]\n\nMeasurement in the computational basis gives 0 with probability \\(|\\alpha|^2\\) and 1 with probability \\(|\\beta|^2\\). A measurement produces one classical outcome and generally changes the state.\n\nThe amplitudes may be negative or complex. Their relative phases affect how they interfere when quantum gates are applied. Constructive interference increases an outcome amplitude, while destructive interference decreases or cancels it.\n\nSuperposition is not the same as classical uncertainty. A classical mixture can have the same measurement probabilities as a quantum superposition in one basis, but it lacks the coherence that enables phase-sensitive interference.\n\nThe Hadamard gate is a basic operation for creating superposition:\n\n\\[\nH|0\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\qquad\nH|1\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nUnderstanding superposition is necessary for understanding quantum interference, quantum circuits, and quantum algorithms.\n\n---\n\n# 12. Formula sheet\n\nGeneral qubit superposition:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nNormalization:\n\n\\[\n|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n\\]\n\nMeasurement probabilities:\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\nHadamard transformations:\n\n\\[\nH|0\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nH|1\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nEqual superposition states:\n\n\\[\n|+\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\qquad\n|-\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nInterference probability:\n\n\\[\n|a+b|^2=|a|^2+|b|^2+a^*b+ab^*.\n\\]\n\nBloch-sphere form:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\n\\cos\\left(\\frac\\theta2\\right)|0\\rangle+\n e^{i\\phi}\\sin\\left(\\frac\\theta2\\right)|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n# 13. Review questions and exercises\n\n## Conceptual questions\n\n1. What is meant by superposition?\n2. Write the general mathematical form of a one-qubit superposition.\n3. What is the difference between a probability amplitude and a probability?\n4. Why can amplitudes be negative or complex even though probabilities cannot?\n5. What is the role of relative phase?\n6. Explain why \\(|+\\rangle\\) and \\(|-\\rangle\\) are different states.\n7. What does a computational-basis measurement do to a superposition?\n8. Why is superposition not the same as classical uncertainty?\n9. What is the role of the Hadamard gate?\n10. Why does a quantum algorithm need interference in addition to superposition?\n\n## Numerical exercises\n\n1. Calculate the measurement probabilities for\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{3}{5}|0\\rangle+\\frac{4}{5}|1\\rangle.\n   \\]\n\n2. Normalize the state\n   \\[\n   |\\phi\\rangle=2|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle.\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate the probabilities for\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}|0\\rangle-\\frac{i}{\\sqrt2}|1\\rangle.\n   \\]\n\n4. Apply the Hadamard gate to \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\n5. Show that \\(H^2=I\\).\n\n6. Calculate the result of applying \\(H\\)-\\(Z\\)-\\(H\\) to \\(|0\\rangle\\).\n\n7. Determine whether\n   \\[\n   |\\phi\\rangle=\\frac{1}{2}|0\\rangle+\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}|1\\rangle\n   \\]\n   is normalized and calculate its measurement probabilities.\n\n8. Explain mathematically why the states \\(|+\\rangle\\) and \\(|-\\rangle\\) have the same computational-basis probabilities.\n\n## Comparison exercise\n\nCreate density matrices for the following two cases:\n\n1. The pure state \\(|+\\rangle\\).\n2. An equal classical mixture of \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\nCompare their diagonal and off-diagonal entries. Explain why a Hadamard gate distinguishes the two cases.\n\n## Programming activity\n\nUsing Qiskit, Cirq, PennyLane, or another quantum programming framework:\n\n1. Prepare \\(|0\\rangle\\) and apply a Hadamard gate.\n2. Measure the circuit 1,000 times.\n3. Compare the measured frequencies with the theoretical probabilities.\n4. Apply two Hadamard gates and verify that the output is almost always 0.\n5. Apply the sequence \\(H\\)-\\(Z\\)-\\(H\\) and verify that the output is almost always 1.\n6. Compare the measurement results of \\(|+\\rangle\\) and \\(|-\\rangle\\) before and after a final Hadamard gate.\n\n---\n\n# 14. Glossary\n\n**Amplitude:** A real or complex coefficient associated with a basis state. Its squared magnitude contributes to a measurement probability.\n\n**Born rule:** The rule stating that the probability of a quantum measurement outcome is the squared magnitude of its amplitude.\n\n**Classical uncertainty:** Lack of knowledge about which definite classical state is present.\n\n**Constructive interference:** Interference that increases an amplitude or outcome probability.\n\n**Destructive interference:** Interference that decreases or cancels an amplitude.\n\n**Measurement:** The process of producing a classical outcome from a quantum state.\n\n**Probability amplitude:** A coefficient in a quantum state whose squared magnitude determines a measurement probability.\n\n**Relative phase:** The phase difference between components of a superposition.\n\n**Superposition:** A linear combination of quantum basis states.\n\n---\n\n# References\n\n[1]: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information \"IBM Quantum Learning: Basics of Quantum Information\"\n\n[2]: https://qiskit.qotlabs.org/learning/courses/quantum-computing-in-practice \"IBM Quantum Learning: Quantum Computing in Practice\"\n\n[3]: https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.68.1049 \"Quantum Computation and Quantum Information by Michael A. Nielsen and Isaac L. Chuang\"\n\n[4]: https://theory.caltech.edu/~preskill/ph229/ \"John Preskill: Lecture Notes for Physics 229, Quantum Information and Computation\"\n\n[5]: https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.68.1049 \"Quantum Computation and Quantum Information by Michael A. Nielsen and Isaac L. Chuang\"\n\n## Key formulas: derivation and interpretation\n\n### Equal superposition\n\n\\[\n|+\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]\n\n**Derivation:** Begin with equal amplitudes \\(a\\) for both basis states:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=a|0\\rangle+a|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nNormalization requires \\(|a|^2+|a|^2=1\\), so \\(2|a|^2=1\\), giving \\(|a|=1/\\sqrt{2}\\). Choosing the positive real amplitude gives the equal-superposition state above.\n\n### Measurement probabilities\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\left|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right|^2=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\left|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right|^2=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nThe probabilities are equal because the two amplitudes have equal magnitudes. The amplitudes themselves, including their signs or phases, remain important for later interference.\n\n### How the Chapter 3 formulas are obtained\n\n#### Deriving the equal superposition\n\n1. Assume the two basis states have equal amplitude \\(a\\): \\(|\\psi\\rangle=a|0\\rangle+a|1\\rangle\\).\n2. Apply normalization: \\(|a|^2+|a|^2=1\\).\n3. Combine equal terms: \\(2|a|^2=1\\).\n4. Divide by 2: \\(|a|^2=1/2\\).\n5. Choose the positive real representative \\(a=1/\\sqrt2\\).\n6. Therefore \\(|+\\rangle=(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\).\n\n#### Deriving its measurement probabilities\n\n1. Identify both amplitudes as \\(1/\\sqrt2\\).\n2. Square each magnitude: \\(|1/\\sqrt2|^2=1/2\\).\n3. Hence each outcome occurs with probability 1/2.\n4. The signs or phases do not change this immediate computational-basis probability, but they affect later interference.\n\n**Variables:** \\(a\\) is the common amplitude; \\(|+\\rangle\\) is the equal-positive superposition; \\(P(0),P(1)\\) are measurement probabilities.\n\n## Chapter assessment\n\n### One-mark questions and answers\n\n1. **Question:** What is superposition?\n   **Answer:** A linear combination of basis states.\n\n2. **Question:** Are amplitudes probabilities?\n   **Answer:** No. Probabilities are squared magnitudes of amplitudes.\n\n3. **Question:** What happens when a superposition is measured?\n   **Answer:** One basis outcome is obtained according to its probability.\n\n4. **Question:** What is relative phase?\n   **Answer:** The phase difference between components of a superposition.\n\n5. **Question:** Why is superposition useful?\n   **Answer:** Gates can transform its amplitudes before measurement.\n\n### Chapter quiz\n\n1. What is superposition?\n\n2. Are amplitudes probabilities?\n\n3. What happens when a superposition is measured?\n\n4. What is relative phase?\n\n5. Why is superposition useful?\n\n### Quiz answer key\n\n1. **A linear combination of basis states.**\n\n2. **No. Probabilities are squared magnitudes of amplitudes.**\n\n3. **One basis outcome is obtained according to its probability.**\n\n4. **The phase difference between components of a superposition.**\n\n5. **Gates can transform its amplitudes before measurement.**\n\n---"
