// Module 2, Chapter 5: Quantum Measurement
// Complete source chapter preserved verbatim as Markdown.

export const chapter5 = {
  id: "module2-chapter5",
  moduleId: "module2",
  title: "Quantum Measurement",
  format: "markdown",
  content: "# Chapter 5: Quantum Measurement\n\n## 5.1 Chapter overview\n\n**Quantum measurement** is the process by which information stored in a quantum state is converted into a classical result. Quantum gates transform quantum states, but measurement produces the values that a classical computer, experimenter, or user can record.\n\nMeasurement is different from an ordinary classical read operation. In the ideal classical model, reading a bit does not change its value. In quantum mechanics, measurement generally changes the state. A superposition can produce one of several possible outcomes, and after the measurement the state is associated with the outcome that was observed.\n\nFor a qubit in the state\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nmeasurement in the computational basis gives\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\nThe probabilities add to one because the state is normalized:\n\n\\[\n|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n\\]\n\nThis chapter explains what measurement means, how computational-basis measurement works, how state collapse is described, why repeated measurements are necessary, and how to interpret experimental outcomes.\n\n---\n\n## 5.2 Learning objectives\n\nBy the end of this chapter, learners should be able to:\n\n1. Define quantum measurement and explain its role in a quantum computation.\n2. Describe measurement in the computational basis.\n3. Calculate measurement probabilities from amplitudes.\n4. Use projection operators to express measurement mathematically.\n5. Explain state collapse after an observed outcome.\n6. Distinguish a single measurement outcome from an outcome distribution.\n7. Explain why quantum experiments are repeated many times.\n8. Interpret measurement counts and frequencies.\n9. Describe measurements in other bases.\n10. Apply the measurement rules to one- and multiple-qubit examples.\n\n---\n\n# 6. What is quantum measurement?\n\n## 6.1 Measurement as quantum-to-classical conversion\n\nA quantum computer processes information in qubits, which are described by state vectors or density matrices. A classical computer cannot directly read a complete quantum state. Measurement is the interface that converts part of the quantum information into classical data.\n\nA typical quantum computation has the structure\n\n\\[\n\\text{initialization}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{quantum gates}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{measurement}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{classical output}.\n\\]\n\nThe quantum state before measurement may contain a superposition of many basis states. The measurement produces one outcome according to a probability distribution determined by the state and the selected measurement basis.\n\n## 6.2 Measurement is probabilistic\n\nSuppose a qubit is in the state\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}|0\\rangle+\\frac12|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nA computational-basis measurement gives\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\frac34,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\frac14.\n\\]\n\nThe result of one measurement cannot be predicted with certainty, even though the probabilities are precisely defined. A single run gives either 0 or 1. Repeated runs reveal the distribution.\n\nQuantum probability does not necessarily mean that the apparatus is merely lacking information about a pre-existing classical value. The probabilities are part of the quantum-mechanical description of the state.\n\n## 6.3 Measurement depends on the basis\n\nA measurement is not specified completely until the measurement basis is given. A state may be a superposition in one basis and a basis state in another.\n\nFor example,\n\n\\[\n|+\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\]\n\nproduces 0 and 1 with equal probabilities in the computational basis. But in the basis \\(\\{|+\\rangle,|-\\rangle\\}\\), it is a definite basis state and produces \\(|+\\rangle\\) with probability 1.\n\nThus, measurement asks a particular question about the quantum state. Different measurement bases reveal different properties.\n\n## 6.4 Measurement is generally irreversible\n\nA unitary gate can be reversed by applying its inverse. Measurement is generally not reversible because the result does not contain the complete information about the pre-measurement superposition.\n\nFor example, both\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\]\n\ngive equal computational-basis probabilities. After measurement, the phase information that distinguished them is generally lost.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Measurement in the computational basis\n\n## 7.1 The computational basis\n\nFor one qubit, the computational basis is\n\n\\[\n\\{|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor two qubits, it is\n\n\\[\n\\{|00\\rangle,|01\\rangle,|10\\rangle,|11\\rangle\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\) qubits, the basis contains all binary strings of length \\(n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\{|x\\rangle:x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\}.\n\\]\n\nComputational-basis measurement reports one of these binary strings.\n\n## 7.2 One-qubit measurement rule\n\nLet\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nA computational-basis measurement has two possible outcomes:\n\n| Outcome | Probability | State after outcome |\n|---|---:|---|\n| 0 | \\(|\\alpha|^2\\) | \\(|0\\rangle\\) |\n| 1 | \\(|\\beta|^2\\) | \\(|1\\rangle\\) |\n\nThe normalization condition guarantees\n\n\\[\n|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n\\]\n\n## 7.3 Projection operators\n\nThe projectors onto the two computational-basis states are\n\n\\[\nP_0=|0\\rangle\\langle0|,\n\\qquad\nP_1=|1\\rangle\\langle1|.\n\\]\n\nIn matrix form,\n\n\\[\nP_0=\n\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix},\n\\qquad\nP_1=\n\\begin{bmatrix}0&0\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThey satisfy\n\n\\[\nP_0+P_1=I,\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nP_0P_1=P_1P_0=0.\n\\]\n\nFor a state \\(|\\psi\\rangle\\), the probability of outcome \\(j\\) is\n\n\\[\nP(j)=\\langle\\psi|P_j|\\psi\\rangle.\n\\]\n\n## 7.4 Deriving the probability of outcome 0\n\nLet\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe probability of outcome 0 is\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\langle\\psi|P_0|\\psi\\rangle\n=\\langle\\psi|0\\rangle\\langle0|\\psi\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nSince\n\n\\[\n\\langle0|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha,\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\langle\\psi|0\\rangle=\\alpha^*,\n\\]\n\nwe get\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\alpha^*\\alpha=|\\alpha|^2.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\[\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\n## 7.5 Measurement in vector form\n\nIf\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{bmatrix},\n\\]\n\nthen computational-basis measurement selects the first or second component according to its squared magnitude:\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\n\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{bmatrix}\\right|^2\n=|\\alpha|^2,\n\\]\n\n\\[\nP(1)=\n\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}0&1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{bmatrix}\\right|^2\n=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n# 8. Measurement probabilities\n\n## 8.1 The Born rule\n\nThe **Born rule** states that the probability of obtaining a particular measurement outcome is the squared magnitude of the corresponding probability amplitude.\n\nFor a one-qubit state,\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nwe have\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\nFor an \\(n\\)-qubit state,\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_{x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}\\alpha_x|x\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nthe probability of measuring the bit string \\(x\\) is\n\n\\[\nP(x)=|\\alpha_x|^2.\n\\]\n\n## 8.2 Complex amplitudes\n\nIf an amplitude is complex, use its magnitude squared. For\n\n\\[\nz=a+ib,\n\\]\n\nwe have\n\n\\[\n|z|^2=z^*z=a^2+b^2.\n\\]\n\nFor example,\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1+i}{2}|0\\rangle+\\frac{1-i}{2}|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\left|\\frac{1+i}{2}\\right|^2=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\left|\\frac{1-i}{2}\\right|^2=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\n## 8.3 Multiple-qubit probabilities\n\nConsider the two-qubit state\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac12|00\\rangle+\\frac12|01\\rangle\n+\\frac12|10\\rangle+\\frac12|11\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nEach amplitude has magnitude \\(1/2\\), so each outcome has probability\n\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac12\\right|^2=\\frac14.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\nP(00)=P(01)=P(10)=P(11)=\\frac14.\n\\]\n\nNow consider the Bell state\n\n\\[\n|\\Phi^+\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThe probabilities are\n\n\\[\nP(00)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(11)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(01)=P(10)=0.\n\\]\n\nThe outcomes are correlated. The measurement of each individual qubit is random, but the pair of outcomes is restricted to 00 or 11.\n\n## 8.4 Probability of an event rather than one string\n\nSometimes we are interested in a group of outcomes. If \\(S\\) is a set of basis states, then\n\n\\[\nP(S)=\\sum_{x\\in S}|\\alpha_x|^2.\n\\]\n\nFor example, in the uniform two-qubit state above, the probability of measuring a string whose first bit is 0 is\n\n\\[\nP(00\\text{ or }01)=\\frac14+\\frac14=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nThe probability of measuring equal bits is\n\n\\[\nP(00\\text{ or }11)=\\frac14+\\frac14=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nProbabilities of mutually exclusive outcomes are added after the individual probabilities have been calculated.\n\n---\n\n# 9. State collapse\n\n## 9.1 Meaning of state collapse\n\nIn the projective-measurement description, **state collapse** is the update of a quantum state to the state associated with the observed measurement outcome.\n\nBefore measurement, suppose\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nAfter measurement:\n\n- If the result is 0, the state becomes \\(|0\\rangle\\).\n- If the result is 1, the state becomes \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\nThe probability of each update is determined by the Born rule.\n\n## 9.2 Projection formula\n\nIf outcome \\(j\\) is observed, the post-measurement state is\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_j\\rangle\n=\\frac{P_j|\\psi\\rangle}{\\sqrt{p_j}},\n\\]\n\nwhere\n\n\\[\np_j=\\langle\\psi|P_j|\\psi\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nFor outcome 0,\n\n\\[\nP_0|\\psi\\rangle\n=|0\\rangle\\langle0|\n(\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle)\n=\\alpha|0\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe probability is \\(|\\alpha|^2\\). Dividing by \\(|\\alpha|\\) gives the normalized state \\(|0\\rangle\\), up to an irrelevant phase.\n\n## 9.3 Collapse is conditional on the outcome\n\nThe state update cannot be specified without knowing which outcome occurred. If outcome 0 occurs, the state becomes \\(|0\\rangle\\). If outcome 1 occurs, it becomes \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\nA measurement therefore has two descriptions:\n\n1. **Selective measurement:** the result is recorded and the conditional post-measurement state is known.\n2. **Non-selective measurement:** the result is ignored, and the state is represented as a mixture of possible post-measurement states.\n\nFor a qubit, ignoring the outcome gives\n\n\\[\n\\rho' = P_0\\rho P_0+P_1\\rho P_1.\n\\]\n\nThis removes the off-diagonal coherence in the computational basis.\n\n## 9.4 Measurement is not the same as applying a random gate\n\nA measurement is not simply a random choice between 0 and 1. The outcome probabilities depend on the input state, and the state update removes the components incompatible with the observed result.\n\nFor example, measuring \\(|+\\rangle\\) in the computational basis produces 0 or 1 with equal probability. After the result is known, the state is no longer \\(|+\\rangle\\); it is the corresponding basis state.\n\n## 9.5 Repeatability of ideal measurement\n\nAn ideal projective measurement is repeatable. If a qubit is measured in the computational basis and the result is 0, then a second immediate measurement in the same basis gives 0 with probability 1.\n\nThis follows because after the first measurement the state is \\(|0\\rangle\\), which is an eigenstate of the measurement. The same applies to outcome 1.\n\n---\n\n# 10. Repeated measurement\n\n## 10.1 Why repeat a quantum circuit?\n\nOne execution of a quantum circuit produces one sample from the output probability distribution. To estimate probabilities, the circuit is run repeatedly. Each repeated execution is often called a **shot** or **trial**.\n\nIf a state produces 0 with probability \\(p\\), then after \\(N\\) independent shots the expected number of zeros is\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{E}[K]=Np.\n\\]\n\nThe observed count fluctuates around this value.\n\n## 10.2 Frequency estimate\n\nIf outcome 0 appears \\(K\\) times in \\(N\\) shots, the observed frequency is\n\n\\[\n\\hat p=\\frac{K}{N}.\n\\]\n\nAs \\(N\\) increases, \\(\\hat p\\) generally approaches the theoretical probability \\(p\\). This is the law of large numbers.\n\nFor example, if \\(p=0.75\\) and the circuit is run 1,000 times, the expected number of zeros is 750. The actual count might be 738, 751, or 766 due to statistical fluctuations.\n\n## 10.3 Binomial distribution\n\nFor a single outcome with probability \\(p\\), the number of occurrences \\(K\\) in \\(N\\) independent shots follows a binomial distribution:\n\n\\[\nP(K=k)=\\binom Nk p^k(1-p)^{N-k}.\n\\]\n\nThe mean is\n\n\\[\n\\mu=Np,\n\\]\n\nand the variance is\n\n\\[\n\\sigma^2=Np(1-p).\n\\]\n\nThe standard deviation is\n\n\\[\n\\sigma=\\sqrt{Np(1-p)}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(N=1,000\\) and \\(p=0.75\\),\n\n\\[\n\\mu=750,\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\sigma=\\sqrt{1000(0.75)(0.25)}\n\\approx13.69.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, a count near 750 is expected, with random variation of roughly several tens of shots.\n\n## 10.4 Standard error of a frequency\n\nThe standard error of the estimated probability is approximately\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{SE}(\\hat p)=\n\\sqrt{\\frac{p(1-p)}{N}}.\n\\]\n\nSince the true \\(p\\) is usually unknown, it may be estimated using \\(\\hat p\\):\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{SE}(\\hat p)\\approx\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}{N}}.\n\\]\n\nMore shots reduce statistical uncertainty approximately as \\(1/\\sqrt N\\). To reduce statistical error by a factor of 10, approximately 100 times as many shots are required.\n\n## 10.5 Measurement counts\n\nQuantum software commonly returns a histogram of bit strings. For example:\n\n```text\n00 : 502\n11 : 498\n```\n\nThis result from 1,000 shots is consistent with the Bell state\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThe ideal probabilities are 0.5 for 00 and 0.5 for 11, while 01 and 10 have probability 0.\n\n## 10.6 Ideal versus experimental outcomes\n\nIn a real device, counts may include outcomes that have zero probability in the ideal mathematical circuit. Causes include:\n\n- Gate errors.\n- Readout errors.\n- Decoherence.\n- Crosstalk between qubits.\n- Imperfect state preparation.\n- Calibration drift.\n\nThe measured distribution is therefore an estimate affected by both quantum randomness and hardware noise.\n\n---\n\n# 11. Measurement outcomes\n\n## 11.1 Outcome versus probability distribution\n\nAn **outcome** is the result of one measurement. A **probability distribution** describes the relative likelihood of all possible outcomes across repeated measurements.\n\nFor\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\sqrt{0.8}|0\\rangle+\\sqrt{0.2}|1\\rangle,\n\\]\n\none measurement returns either 0 or 1. The complete theoretical distribution is\n\n\\[\nP(0)=0.8,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=0.2.\n\\]\n\nIt is incorrect to say that one run produces “0.8.” Probabilities describe an ensemble of possible runs, not the classical output of one shot.\n\n## 11.2 Deterministic outcomes\n\nA measurement is deterministic in a basis when one outcome has probability 1. The states \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(|1\\rangle\\) are deterministic in the computational basis:\n\n\\[\nP_{|0\\rangle}(0)=1,\n\\qquad\nP_{|1\\rangle}(1)=1.\n\\]\n\nA state can be deterministic in one basis and probabilistic in another. For example, \\(|+\\rangle\\) is deterministic in the \\(\\{|+\\rangle,|-\\rangle\\}\\) basis but probabilistic in the computational basis.\n\n## 11.3 Correlated outcomes\n\nFor entangled states, individual outcomes may be random while joint outcomes are correlated. For the Bell state\n\n\\[\n|\\Phi^+\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\]\n\nmeasuring the first qubit gives 0 or 1 with equal probability. Once the first result is known, the second result is correlated with it.\n\nThe outcome probabilities are\n\n\\[\nP(00)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(11)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(01)=P(10)=0.\n\\]\n\n## 11.4 Marginal probabilities\n\nFor a multi-qubit system, a marginal probability describes one subset of the qubits. For the Bell state,\n\n\\[\nP(q_0=0)=P(00)+P(01)=\\frac12,\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nP(q_1=0)=P(00)+P(10)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nThe individual qubits are random, but the joint distribution contains correlations.\n\n## 11.5 Post-processing outcomes\n\nAfter measurement, classical post-processing may group, count, compare, or transform the outcomes. For example, a program may count how often the parity is even:\n\n\\[\n\\text{parity}(b_1b_2)=b_1\\oplus b_2.\n\\]\n\nFor a two-qubit result, 00 and 11 have even parity, while 01 and 10 have odd parity. Quantum algorithms often encode useful information in such classical properties of measurement outcomes.\n\n---\n\n# 12. Measurements in other bases\n\n## 12.1 Hadamard basis\n\nThe Hadamard basis is\n\n\\[\n\\{|+\\rangle,|-\\rangle\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere\n\n\\[\n|+\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\qquad\n|-\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nTo measure in the Hadamard basis using a computational-basis measurement, apply a Hadamard gate first:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Hadamard-basis measurement}=H\\text{ followed by computational measurement}.\n\\]\n\nFor example,\n\n\\[\n|+\\rangle\\xrightarrow{H}|0\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nso a Hadamard-basis measurement of \\(|+\\rangle\\) gives the \\(+\\) outcome with certainty.\n\n## 12.2 Pauli-Z measurement\n\nA computational-basis measurement is also called a measurement of the Pauli-Z observable. The operator is\n\n\\[\nZ=|0\\rangle\\langle0|-|1\\rangle\\langle1|.\n\\]\n\nThe eigenvalues are \\(+1\\) for \\(|0\\rangle\\) and \\(-1\\) for \\(|1\\rangle\\). A measured bit may be mapped to an eigenvalue using\n\n\\[\n0\\mapsto+1,\n\\qquad\n1\\mapsto-1.\n\\]\n\n## 12.3 Pauli-X measurement\n\nAn X-basis measurement distinguishes \\(|+\\rangle\\) and \\(|-\\rangle\\). It can be implemented by applying \\(H\\) and then measuring in the computational basis.\n\nThe expectation value of X for a state \\(\\rho\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\langle X\\rangle=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho X).\n\\]\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\\[\n\\langle Z\\rangle=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho Z).\n\\]\n\nThese expectation values are estimated by repeated measurements.\n\n---\n\n# 13. Measurement operators and the general rule\n\n## 13.1 Projective measurements\n\nA projective measurement is described by projectors \\(P_m\\) satisfying\n\n\\[\nP_m^2=P_m,\n\\qquad\nP_m^\\dagger=P_m,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_mP_m=I.\n\\]\n\nFor a state \\(|\\psi\\rangle\\), the probability of outcome \\(m\\) is\n\n\\[\np_m=\\langle\\psi|P_m|\\psi\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe post-measurement state is\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_m\\rangle=\n\\frac{P_m|\\psi\\rangle}{\\sqrt{p_m}}.\n\\]\n\n## 13.2 Measurement operators\n\nMore generally, a measurement can be described by operators \\(M_m\\) satisfying\n\n\\[\n\\sum_mM_m^\\dagger M_m=I.\n\\]\n\nThe probability of outcome \\(m\\) is\n\n\\[\np_m=\\langle\\psi|M_m^\\dagger M_m|\\psi\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nand the post-measurement state is\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_m\\rangle=\n\\frac{M_m|\\psi\\rangle}{\\sqrt{p_m}}.\n\\]\n\nFor projective computational-basis measurement,\n\n\\[\nM_0=P_0,\n\\qquad\nM_1=P_1.\n\\]\n\n## 13.3 Density-matrix form\n\nIf the state is represented by a density matrix \\(\\rho\\), the probability of outcome \\(m\\) is\n\n\\[\np_m=\\operatorname{Tr}(M_m^\\dagger M_m\\rho).\n\\]\n\nFor a projective measurement, the conditional post-measurement state is\n\n\\[\n\\rho_m=\\frac{P_m\\rho P_m}{p_m}.\n\\]\n\nIf the result is not recorded, the state becomes\n\n\\[\n\\rho'=\\sum_mP_m\\rho P_m.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n# 14. Worked examples\n\n## Example 5.1: One-qubit measurement\n\nLet\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}|0\\rangle+\\frac12|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe probabilities are\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\frac34,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\frac14.\n\\]\n\nIf outcome 0 occurs, the post-measurement state is \\(|0\\rangle\\). If outcome 1 occurs, it is \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\n## Example 5.2: Measurement of a complex state\n\nLet\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{1+i}{2}|0\\rangle+\\frac{1-i}{2}|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe probabilities are\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nThe complex phases do not alter the immediate computational-basis probabilities, but they could affect the result after another gate.\n\n## Example 5.3: Repeated measurements\n\nA circuit produces outcome 1 with theoretical probability \\(p=0.2\\). If it is run \\(N=500\\) times, the expected number of ones is\n\n\\[\nNp=500(0.2)=100.\n\\]\n\nThe standard deviation is\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{Np(1-p)}\n=\\sqrt{500(0.2)(0.8)}\n=\\sqrt{80}\\approx8.94.\n\\]\n\nA count between roughly 90 and 110 is not surprising. The exact interpretation depends on the desired confidence interval and whether hardware noise is present.\n\n## Example 5.4: Bell-state outcomes\n\nFor\n\n\\[\n|\\Phi^+\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\]\n\nmeasurement produces 00 or 11. The probability of each is \\(1/2\\). If 1,000 ideal shots are taken, the counts should be near 500 for each outcome, with 01 and 10 absent in the ideal model.\n\n## Example 5.5: Measurement after a Hadamard gate\n\nStart with \\(|0\\rangle\\) and apply \\(H\\):\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle\\xrightarrow{H}|+\\rangle\n=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nComputational measurement gives\n\n\\[\nP(0)=P(1)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nIf the result is 0, the state becomes \\(|0\\rangle\\). If the result is 1, the state becomes \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\n## Example 5.6: Measuring in the Hadamard basis\n\nPrepare \\(|+\\rangle\\). To measure in the Hadamard basis, apply \\(H\\):\n\n\\[\n|+\\rangle\\xrightarrow{H}|0\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nA computational-basis measurement then produces 0 with probability 1. In the Hadamard basis, \\(|+\\rangle\\) is a definite state.\n\n---\n\n# 15. Common misconceptions\n\n## Misconception 1: Measurement reveals the complete quantum state\n\nA single measurement produces one outcome. It does not reveal all amplitudes or phases of the state.\n\n## Misconception 2: A probability is the output of one measurement\n\nA probability describes the distribution across repeated preparations. A single measurement produces one discrete outcome.\n\n## Misconception 3: Measurement merely reads a pre-existing classical value\n\nFor a superposition, the measurement outcome is described probabilistically, and the state is generally changed by the measurement.\n\n## Misconception 4: The state always collapses to \\(|0\\rangle\\) or \\(|1\\rangle\\)\n\nIt collapses to an eigenstate of the selected measurement basis. If a different basis is measured, the post-measurement state belongs to that basis.\n\n## Misconception 5: More shots remove all uncertainty\n\nMore shots reduce statistical uncertainty but do not remove hardware noise or systematic errors. They also cannot reconstruct arbitrary quantum information without an appropriate tomography procedure.\n\n## Misconception 6: Entangled outcomes are individually deterministic\n\nAn entangled state can have random individual outcomes and deterministic correlations between outcomes.\n\n## Misconception 7: Zero ideal probability means zero experimental count\n\nOn noisy hardware, errors can create outcomes that have zero probability in the ideal circuit.\n\n---\n\n# 16. Chapter summary\n\nQuantum measurement converts quantum information into classical information. A measurement must specify the basis or measurement operators.\n\nFor computational-basis measurement of\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle,\n\\]\n\noutcome 0 occurs with probability\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2,\n\\]\n\nand outcome 1 occurs with probability\n\n\\[\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\nAfter measurement, the state is updated to the state associated with the observed outcome. This is called state collapse in the projective-measurement description. An ideal measurement is repeatable: an immediate second measurement in the same basis gives the same result.\n\nOne circuit execution produces one sample. Repeated measurements estimate the probability distribution. The expected count of an outcome is \\(Np\\), and the standard deviation for a binomial count is\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{Np(1-p)}.\n\\]\n\nFor multiple qubits, measurement produces a bit string. Entangled states may exhibit strong correlations even when each individual result is random.\n\nMeasurement in another basis can be implemented by applying a basis-changing unitary before a computational-basis measurement. Quantum algorithms delay measurement because early measurement can destroy the superposition and interference needed for computation.\n\n---\n\n# 17. Formula sheet\n\nOne-qubit measurement probabilities:\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\nNormalization:\n\n\\[\n|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n\\]\n\nProjectors:\n\n\\[\nP_0=|0\\rangle\\langle0|,\n\\qquad\nP_1=|1\\rangle\\langle1|.\n\\]\n\nProjective probability rule:\n\n\\[\np_j=\\langle\\psi|P_j|\\psi\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nPost-measurement state:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_j\\rangle=\n\\frac{P_j|\\psi\\rangle}{\\sqrt{p_j}}.\n\\]\n\nGeneral measurement probability:\n\n\\[\np_m=\\langle\\psi|M_m^\\dagger M_m|\\psi\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nGeneral post-measurement state:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_m\\rangle=\n\\frac{M_m|\\psi\\rangle}{\\sqrt{p_m}}.\n\\]\n\nExpected count:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{E}[K]=Np.\n\\]\n\nBinomial probability:\n\n\\[\nP(K=k)=\\binom Nk p^k(1-p)^{N-k}.\n\\]\n\nBinomial standard deviation:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma=\\sqrt{Np(1-p)}.\n\\]\n\nFrequency estimate:\n\n\\[\n\\hat p=\\frac KN.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n# 18. Review questions and exercises\n\n## Conceptual questions\n\n1. What is quantum measurement?\n2. Why is measurement needed in a quantum computation?\n3. What is the computational basis for one qubit?\n4. State the Born rule for a one-qubit measurement.\n5. What happens to a state after a computational-basis measurement?\n6. Why is measurement generally irreversible?\n7. What is state collapse?\n8. Why are quantum circuits run repeatedly?\n9. What is the difference between one measurement outcome and a probability distribution?\n10. Why can an entangled state have random individual outcomes but correlated joint outcomes?\n11. How can a computational-basis measurement be used to measure in the Hadamard basis?\n12. What types of errors can affect real measurement counts?\n\n## Numerical exercises\n\n1. Calculate the measurement probabilities for\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{3}{5}|0\\rangle+\\frac{4}{5}|1\\rangle.\n   \\]\n\n2. Determine the post-measurement state for each possible result of\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n   \\]\n\n3. Find the probabilities of all computational-basis outcomes for\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac12(|00\\rangle+|01\\rangle+|10\\rangle+|11\\rangle).\n   \\]\n\n4. Find the probabilities for the Bell state\n   \\[\n   |\\Phi^+\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n   \\]\n\n5. A circuit produces outcome 0 with probability 0.6. For 2,000 shots, calculate the expected count and standard deviation.\n\n6. If outcome 1 appears 480 times in 1,000 shots, calculate the observed frequency.\n\n7. Explain why \\(|+\\rangle\\) gives a deterministic result after a Hadamard gate followed by computational-basis measurement.\n\n8. Write the projectors \\(P_0\\) and \\(P_1\\) as matrices and verify that \\(P_0+P_1=I\\).\n\n9. Show that the probabilities of all outcomes of a normalized two-qubit state sum to 1.\n\n10. For a Bell state, calculate the marginal probability that the first qubit is 0.\n\n## Measurement-analysis activity\n\nRun a circuit for 1, 10, 100, 1,000, and 10,000 shots. For each shot count:\n\n1. Record the counts of 0 and 1.\n2. Calculate the observed frequency.\n3. Compare it with the theoretical probability.\n4. Plot the difference between observed and theoretical frequency.\n5. Explain why the statistical error generally decreases as the number of shots increases.\n\n## Programming activity\n\nUsing Qiskit, Cirq, PennyLane, or another quantum programming framework:\n\n1. Prepare \\(|0\\rangle\\) and measure it.\n2. Apply \\(X\\) to \\(|0\\rangle\\) and measure it.\n3. Apply \\(H\\) to \\(|0\\rangle\\) and measure 1,000 shots.\n4. Prepare a Bell state and record the two-qubit measurement counts.\n5. Apply a final Hadamard gate to one qubit of the Bell circuit and analyze the new outcomes.\n6. Repeat the experiments with a noisy simulator if available and compare ideal and noisy distributions.\n\n---\n\n# 19. Glossary\n\n**Born rule:** The rule that assigns a probability equal to the squared magnitude of a measurement amplitude.\n\n**Computational basis:** The basis \\(\\{|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle\\}\\) for one qubit and the corresponding binary-string basis for multiple qubits.\n\n**Measurement:** The process of converting quantum information into a classical outcome.\n\n**Measurement basis:** The set of states whose outcomes are distinguished by a measurement.\n\n**Measurement outcome:** The classical result obtained in one measurement run.\n\n**Projector:** An operator that selects the component of a state associated with a measurement subspace.\n\n**Repeated measurement:** Running the same state-preparation and measurement procedure many times to estimate probabilities.\n\n**State collapse:** The update of a quantum state to the state associated with the observed measurement outcome.\n\n**Shot:** One execution of a quantum circuit followed by measurement.\n\n---\n\n# References\n\n[1]: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information \"IBM Quantum Learning: Basics of Quantum Information\"\n\n[2]: https://qiskit.qotlabs.org/learning/courses/quantum-computing-in-practice \"IBM Quantum Learning: Quantum Computing in Practice\"\n\n[3]: https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.68.1049 \"Quantum Computation and Quantum Information by Michael A. Nielsen and Isaac L. Chuang\"\n\n[4]: https://theory.caltech.edu/~preskill/ph229/ \"John Preskill: Lecture Notes for Physics 229, Quantum Information and Computation\"\n\n[5]: https://www.nist.gov/quantum-information-science \"National Institute of Standards and Technology: Quantum Information Science\"\n\n## Key formulas: derivation and interpretation\n\n### Born rule in the computational basis\n\nFor\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nmeasurement gives:\n\n\\[\nP(0)=|\\alpha|^2,\\qquad P(1)=|\\beta|^2.\n\\]\n\n**Derivation:** Projectors for the computational basis are \\(\\Pi_0=|0\\rangle\\langle0|\\) and \\(\\Pi_1=|1\\rangle\\langle1|\\). The probability of outcome \\(j\\) is \\(P(j)=\\langle\\psi|\\Pi_j|\\psi\\rangle\\). For outcome 0:\n\n\\[\n\\langle\\psi|0\\rangle\\langle0|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha^*\\alpha=|\\alpha|^2.\n\\]\n\nThe same calculation gives \\(P(1)=|\\beta|^2\\).\n\n### Post-measurement state\n\nAfter observing outcome \\(j\\), the state becomes:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_j\\rangle=\\frac{\\Pi_j|\\psi\\rangle}{\\sqrt{P(j)}}.\n\\]\n\nThe denominator normalizes the projected state, and \\(\\Pi_j\\) removes all components inconsistent with the observed outcome.\n\n### How the Chapter 5 formulas are obtained\n\n#### Deriving the Born rule using projectors\n\n1. For computational-basis outcome 0, define \\(\\Pi_0=|0\\rangle\\langle0|\\); for outcome 1, define \\(\\Pi_1=|1\\rangle\\langle1|\\).\n2. The probability rule for projector \\(\\Pi_j\\) is \\(P(j)=\\langle\\psi|\\Pi_j|\\psi\\rangle\\).\n3. Substitute \\(|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle\\) into the outcome-0 expression.\n4. Orthogonality gives \\(\\langle0|0\\rangle=1\\) and \\(\\langle0|1\\rangle=0\\).\n5. Therefore \\(\\Pi_0|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0\\rangle\\), and the inner product gives \\(P(0)=\\alpha^*\\alpha=|\\alpha|^2\\).\n6. The same steps give \\(P(1)=|\\beta|^2\\).\n\n#### Deriving the post-measurement state\n\n1. Apply the projector to remove components inconsistent with the observed result.\n2. The projected vector may not have unit norm.\n3. Its norm is \\(\\sqrt{P(j)}\\).\n4. Divide by that norm to obtain \\(|\\psi_j\\rangle=\\Pi_j|\\psi\\rangle/\\sqrt{P(j)}\\).\n\n**Variables:** \\(\\Pi_j\\) is a measurement projector; \\(j\\) labels the result; \\(P(j)\\) is its probability; \\(|\\psi_j\\rangle\\) is the post-measurement state.\n\n## Chapter assessment\n\n### One-mark questions and answers\n\n1. **Question:** What is quantum measurement?\n   **Answer:** Conversion of quantum information into a classical outcome.\n\n2. **Question:** What is the computational basis for one qubit?\n   **Answer:** {|0⟩, |1⟩}.\n\n3. **Question:** What is state collapse?\n   **Answer:** Projection of the state into the observed result.\n\n4. **Question:** Why are measurements repeated?\n   **Answer:** To estimate outcome probabilities statistically.\n\n5. **Question:** Does measurement generally disturb a quantum state?\n   **Answer:** Yes.\n\n### Chapter quiz\n\n1. What is quantum measurement?\n\n2. What is the computational basis for one qubit?\n\n3. What is state collapse?\n\n4. Why are measurements repeated?\n\n5. Does measurement generally disturb a quantum state?\n\n### Quiz answer key\n\n1. **Conversion of quantum information into a classical outcome.**\n\n2. **{|0⟩, |1⟩}.**\n\n3. **Projection of the state into the observed result.**\n\n4. **To estimate outcome probabilities statistically.**\n\n5. **Yes.**\n\n---\n"
};


