export const chapter7 = {
  id: "module2-chapter7",
  moduleId: "module2",
  title: "Quantum Circuits",
  format: "markdown",
  content: "# Chapter 7: Quantum Circuits\n\n## 7.1 Chapter overview\n\nA **quantum circuit** is an ordered arrangement of quantum gates, qubit wires, and measurement operations used to perform a quantum computation. It provides a visual and mathematical description of how qubits are initialized, transformed, entangled, and measured.\n\nA simple quantum circuit follows the pattern\n\n\\[\n\\text{initialize}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{apply gates}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{measure}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{record classical output}.\n\\]\n\nIn a circuit diagram:\n\n- Horizontal lines represent qubits.\n- Time flows from left to right.\n- Boxes or symbols represent quantum gates.\n- Connected multi-wire symbols represent multi-qubit gates.\n- Measurement symbols convert quantum states into classical bits.\n- Classical wires carry measurement results to classical registers or control logic.\n\nThe same circuit has both a graphical and an algebraic description. If gates \\(U_1,U_2,\\ldots,U_k\\) are applied in sequence, the overall unitary transformation is\n\n\\[\nU=U_kU_{k-1}\\cdots U_2U_1.\n\\]\n\nThe rightmost matrix acts first, even though the circuit is usually read from left to right in time.\n\nThis chapter introduces circuit notation, qubits and wires, gate sequences, measurement, circuit depth, and the practical execution of circuits on simulators and quantum hardware.\n\n---\n\n## 7.2 Learning objectives\n\nAfter studying this chapter, learners should be able to:\n\n1. Define a quantum circuit.\n2. Identify the purpose of qubit wires and circuit moments.\n3. Read common one- and two-qubit circuit symbols.\n4. Translate a circuit into matrix notation.\n5. Explain why gate order matters.\n6. Distinguish quantum wires from classical wires.\n7. Place measurement operations correctly in a circuit.\n8. Define circuit width, gate count, and circuit depth.\n9. Explain how a circuit is executed repeatedly to estimate output probabilities.\n10. Analyze simple circuits and predict their measurement outcomes.\n\n---\n\n# 8. What is a quantum circuit?\n\n## 8.1 Definition\n\nA **quantum circuit** is a finite sequence of quantum operations acting on one or more qubits. The circuit begins with an initial state, usually \\(|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}\\), and ends with one or more measurements.\n\nA circuit can be represented abstractly as\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_0\\rangle\n\\xrightarrow{U_1}\n|\\psi_1\\rangle\n\\xrightarrow{U_2}\n\\cdots\n\\xrightarrow{U_k}\n|\\psi_k\\rangle\n\\xrightarrow{\\text{measurement}}\n\\text{classical bits}.\n\\]\n\nA circuit is therefore both:\n\n- A **program** for a quantum processor.\n- A **mathematical expression** for a sequence of transformations.\n\n## 8.2 Why circuits are used\n\nQuantum circuits help us understand and communicate quantum algorithms. They show:\n\n- Which qubits are involved.\n- Which gates act on each qubit.\n- When gates are applied.\n- Which qubits become entangled.\n- When measurement occurs.\n- How classical control may depend on a measurement result.\n\nCircuit diagrams are especially useful because the state of many qubits can become mathematically complicated. A diagram provides a compact description of the operations without writing the full state vector after every step.\n\n## 8.3 Initial state\n\nUnless stated otherwise, an \\(n\\)-qubit circuit is commonly initialized in\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}\n=|0\\rangle|0\\rangle\\cdots|0\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nFor two qubits,\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle^{\\otimes2}=|00\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe initialization assumption must be stated because the result of a circuit depends on its input state.\n\n## 8.4 Circuit example\n\nThe following circuit prepares a Bell state:\n\n```text\nq0: ──H────●────M──\n           │\nq1: ───────X────M──\n```\n\nIt performs the following operations:\n\n1. Start with \\(|00\\rangle\\).\n2. Apply \\(H\\) to qubit 0.\n3. Apply CNOT with qubit 0 as control and qubit 1 as target.\n4. Measure both qubits.\n\nThe final quantum state before measurement is\n\n\\[\n|\\Phi^+\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThe possible measurement outcomes are 00 and 11, each with probability \\(1/2\\).\n\n---\n\n# 9. Circuit notation\n\n## 9.1 Wires and direction of time\n\nQuantum wires are drawn as horizontal lines. The conventional direction of time is from left to right.\n\n```text\nq: ───────────────\n    time flows →\n```\n\nThe wire does not represent a physical wire carrying a classical voltage. It represents the path of a qubit through the sequence of operations.\n\nA wire may carry a qubit through several gates. The qubit remains a quantum system until it is measured or otherwise transferred into a classical record.\n\n## 9.2 Single-qubit gates\n\nA single-qubit gate is drawn as a box on one wire:\n\n```text\nq: ──H──X──Z──\n```\n\nThis means the circuit applies \\(H\\), then \\(X\\), then \\(Z\\) to the same qubit.\n\nThe corresponding state transformation is\n\n\\[\n|\\psi'\\rangle=ZXH|\\psi\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe circuit is read from left to right, while the matrix product is written with the first operation on the right.\n\n## 9.3 Common gate symbols\n\nTypical symbols include:\n\n```text\nX gate:       ──X──\nY gate:       ──Y──\nZ gate:       ──Z──\nH gate:       ──H──\nS gate:       ──S──\nT gate:       ──T──\n```\n\nA gate box identifies the operation applied to the qubit at that point in the circuit.\n\n## 9.4 Multi-qubit gates\n\nA multi-qubit gate connects two or more wires. The CNOT is commonly written as\n\n```text\ncontrol: ──●──\n           │\ntarget:  ──X──\n```\n\nThe filled dot identifies the control qubit. The X symbol identifies the target operation. The target flips only when the control is in state 1.\n\nA SWAP gate is shown using two crossing symbols:\n\n```text\nq0: ──×──\n       │\nq1: ──×──\n```\n\nIt exchanges the states of two qubits:\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{SWAP}|a,b\\rangle=|b,a\\rangle.\n\\]\n\n## 9.5 Measurement symbols\n\nA measurement may be shown as\n\n```text\nq: ──H──M──\n```\n\nor with a meter-like symbol in a circuit drawing. The measurement converts the qubit to a classical result.\n\nFor multiple qubits,\n\n```text\nq0: ──H──M──\nq1: ──X──M──\n```\n\nmeans that both qubits are measured.\n\n## 9.6 Classical wires\n\nClassical results are often represented by double lines:\n\n```text\nq: ──H──M──╫──\n           ║\nclassical: ═╩═\n```\n\nThe exact graphical style may vary between software frameworks. The important distinction is conceptual:\n\n- A single line represents a quantum wire.\n- A double line represents a classical wire.\n- Measurement transfers information from a quantum wire to a classical wire.\n\n## 9.7 Barriers and circuit organization\n\nA barrier is a visual marker that separates groups of gates. It may be used to organize a circuit or prevent an optimizer from combining gates across that boundary.\n\n```text\nq: ──H──│──X──\n```\n\nA barrier is not generally a physical quantum operation. It is a structural instruction for circuit visualization or compilation.\n\n---\n\n# 10. Qubits and wires\n\n## 10.1 Qubit wires represent state paths\n\nA wire identifies a qubit as it moves through the circuit. For a circuit with \\(n\\) qubits, there are usually \\(n\\) quantum wires:\n\n```text\nq0: ─────────────\nq1: ─────────────\nq2: ─────────────\n```\n\nThe labels \\(q_0,q_1,q_2\\) identify the qubits. They are not necessarily physical positions on a chip; they are logical labels used by the circuit description.\n\n## 10.2 Wire order and basis-state order\n\nThe order of qubits must be specified when writing a multi-qubit state. For two qubits, the state may be written as\n\n\\[\n|q_0q_1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe basis ordering is commonly\n\n\\[\n|00\\rangle,\n|01\\rangle,\n|10\\rangle,\n|11\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nHowever, software frameworks may use different conventions for displaying bit strings and indexing basis states. A careful implementation must document its qubit order and classical-bit order.\n\n## 10.3 Independent wires and tensor products\n\nIf two gates act independently on two qubits, the combined operation is a tensor product. For example, applying \\(H\\) to the first qubit and \\(X\\) to the second is\n\n\\[\nH\\otimes X.\n\\]\n\nStarting from \\(|00\\rangle\\),\n\n\\[\n(H\\otimes X)|00\\rangle\n=H|0\\rangle\\otimes X|0\\rangle\n=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nExpanding,\n\n\\[\n(H\\otimes X)|00\\rangle\n=\\frac{|01\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\n## 10.4 Wires and entanglement\n\nA wire can carry part of an entangled state. After a multi-qubit gate creates entanglement, it is not correct to assign an independent pure state to each wire.\n\nFor example, after the Bell-state circuit,\n\n\\[\n|\\Phi^+\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2},\n\\]\n\nthe two wires are correlated. The full two-qubit state is required to describe the system.\n\n## 10.5 Logical and physical qubits\n\nA **logical qubit** is an abstract qubit used by an algorithm. A **physical qubit** is a hardware device that realizes a quantum state.\n\nIn a noisy device, a logical circuit may need to be compiled into physical operations. This process may add:\n\n- Additional gates.\n- Qubit-routing operations.\n- Error-correction operations.\n- SWAP gates to satisfy hardware connectivity.\n\nTherefore, the logical circuit depth may be smaller than the physical circuit depth executed by the hardware.\n\n---\n\n# 11. Gate sequence\n\n## 11.1 Sequential application\n\nIf gates \\(U_1,U_2,U_3\\) are applied from left to right, then the state evolves as\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_1\\rangle=U_1|\\psi_0\\rangle,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_2\\rangle=U_2U_1|\\psi_0\\rangle,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_3\\rangle=U_3U_2U_1|\\psi_0\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe total unitary is\n\n\\[\nU_{\\text{total}}=U_3U_2U_1.\n\\]\n\n## 11.2 Gate order matters\n\nQuantum gates generally do not commute:\n\n\\[\nAB\\neq BA.\n\\]\n\nConsider \\(H\\) and \\(Z\\) acting on \\(|0\\rangle\\).\n\nFirst apply H, then Z:\n\n\\[\nZH|0\\rangle=Z|+\\rangle=|-\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nFirst apply Z, then H:\n\n\\[\nHZ|0\\rangle=H|0\\rangle=|+\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe final states are different. Therefore, changing the order of gates changes the circuit.\n\n## 11.3 Parallel gates\n\nGates acting on different qubits can sometimes be applied in the same time step. For example,\n\n```text\nq0: ──H──\nq1: ──X──\n```\n\nhas one parallel layer containing H and X. The combined operation is \\(H\\otimes X\\).\n\nParallelism affects circuit depth. It does not mean that all gates can always be executed at the same time. Hardware connectivity, control electronics, and gate conflicts may impose restrictions.\n\n## 11.4 Circuit moments\n\nA **moment** is a time slice or layer in which gates are scheduled to occur simultaneously, subject to circuit constraints.\n\nFor example,\n\n```text\nq0: ──H────●────M──\n           │\nq1: ──X────X────M──\n```\n\nmay be divided into moments:\n\n1. Moment 1: H on q0 and X on q1.\n2. Moment 2: CNOT on q0 and q1.\n3. Moment 3: measurement.\n\nThe number of gate moments before measurement is a simple form of circuit depth.\n\n## 11.5 Conditional operations\n\nSome circuits use a classical measurement result to decide whether a later gate is applied. This is called classical control or feed-forward.\n\nConceptually,\n\n```text\nq: ──M──╫──\n          ║\nclassical:╚═ control X\n```\n\nThe exact notation varies. The key point is that the later operation depends on a classical result that has already been produced.\n\nQuantum algorithms may also use coherent control, in which a qubit controls another operation without being measured. A controlled-U gate is reversible and preserves quantum coherence.\n\n---\n\n# 12. Measurement operations\n\n## 12.1 Measurement at the end of a circuit\n\nMost introductory circuits measure after the quantum gates have finished:\n\n```text\nq: ──H──Z──H──M──\n```\n\nStarting from \\(|0\\rangle\\), this circuit ends in \\(|1\\rangle\\), so the measurement returns 1 with probability 1.\n\n## 12.2 Measuring one qubit of a multi-qubit state\n\nA circuit may measure only selected qubits. For a two-qubit state,\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\alpha_{00}|00\\rangle+\n\\alpha_{01}|01\\rangle+\n\\alpha_{10}|10\\rangle+\n\\alpha_{11}|11\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nmeasuring only the first qubit groups outcomes according to its value:\n\n\\[\nP(q_0=0)=|\\alpha_{00}|^2+|\\alpha_{01}|^2,\n\\]\n\n\\[\nP(q_0=1)=|\\alpha_{10}|^2+|\\alpha_{11}|^2.\n\\]\n\nThe unmeasured qubit may remain in a conditional quantum state.\n\n## 12.3 Measurement of a Bell state\n\nConsider\n\n\\[\n|\\Phi^+\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nMeasuring only the first qubit gives 0 or 1 with equal probability. If the result is 0, the second qubit is conditionally in \\(|0\\rangle\\). If the result is 1, the second qubit is conditionally in \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\nMeasuring both qubits gives 00 or 11, each with probability \\(1/2\\).\n\n## 12.4 Measurement and classical registers\n\nA quantum circuit may map measured qubits to classical bits. For example:\n\n```text\nq0: ──H──M── c0\nq1: ──X──M── c1\n```\n\nThe classical output can be represented as a bit string such as 00, 01, 10, or 11, depending on the measurement results.\n\nThe mapping between qubit indices and displayed classical-bit order must be checked in the chosen software framework.\n\n## 12.5 Mid-circuit measurement\n\nA **mid-circuit measurement** occurs before the circuit has finished. It produces a classical result and may change the state of the measured qubit. Later gates can be conditioned on that result.\n\nMid-circuit measurement is used in:\n\n- Quantum teleportation.\n- Quantum error correction.\n- Reset operations.\n- Adaptive algorithms.\n- Repeated syndrome extraction.\n\nIt must be distinguished from a coherent controlled operation. A coherent control uses a quantum qubit without measuring it; a classical feed-forward control uses an already measured classical result.\n\n## 12.6 Measurement is not a gate box\n\nA measurement is often drawn at the end of a wire, but mathematically it is not an ordinary unitary gate. It is described by projectors or measurement operators. The measurement output is probabilistic and the state update is generally irreversible.\n\n---\n\n# 13. Circuit depth\n\n## 13.1 Definition\n\n**Circuit depth** is the number of sequential layers of operations in a circuit, assuming gates on independent qubits can be executed in parallel when allowed.\n\nCircuit depth is different from total gate count:\n\n- **Gate count** is the total number of gate operations.\n- **Circuit depth** is the number of time layers needed to schedule them.\n- **Circuit width** is the number of qubits used.\n\n## 13.2 Example of gate count and depth\n\nConsider\n\n```text\nq0: ──H────●────H──\n           │\nq1: ──X────X────Z──\n```\n\nA possible schedule is:\n\n- Layer 1: H on q0 and X on q1.\n- Layer 2: CNOT.\n- Layer 3: H on q0 and Z on q1.\n\nThe circuit has:\n\n- Gate count: 5 operations if CNOT counts as one two-qubit gate.\n- Width: 2 qubits.\n- Gate depth: 3 layers.\n\n## 13.3 Why depth matters\n\nReal qubits lose coherence over time, and gates are imperfect. A deeper circuit generally provides more opportunities for error. Circuit depth therefore affects:\n\n- Probability of successful computation.\n- Total execution time.\n- Required error correction.\n- Number of noisy operations.\n- Feasibility on near-term hardware.\n\nReducing depth is often as important as reducing gate count.\n\n## 13.4 Logical depth versus hardware depth\n\nA logical circuit may contain a CNOT between two logical qubits. If the corresponding physical qubits are not connected, the compiler may insert SWAP gates:\n\n```text\nlogical:  q0 ─────●────\n                  │\n          q1 ─────X────\n```\n\nThe hardware may need routing operations before the CNOT. The physical circuit is then deeper than the logical circuit.\n\n## 13.5 Depth and parallelism\n\nSuppose ten single-qubit gates act on ten different qubits. If the hardware allows all of them simultaneously, they may contribute only one layer to depth. If they must be scheduled one after another, they contribute ten layers.\n\nParallel execution depends on:\n\n- Gates acting on disjoint qubits.\n- Hardware control limitations.\n- Crosstalk constraints.\n- Calibration schedules.\n- Connectivity and routing.\n\n---\n\n# 14. Circuit execution\n\n## 14.1 From circuit description to output\n\nExecuting a quantum circuit involves several stages:\n\n1. Define the circuit and initial state.\n2. Compile or transpile it into the target gate set.\n3. Map logical qubits to physical qubits if using hardware.\n4. Apply the gates.\n5. Measure the selected qubits.\n6. Repeat the circuit for the requested number of shots.\n7. Collect and analyze the classical results.\n\n## 14.2 Simulation\n\nA quantum simulator calculates the state vector or samples from a mathematical model of the circuit. For small circuits, state-vector simulation is convenient. An \\(n\\)-qubit state vector contains \\(2^n\\) amplitudes:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_{x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}\\alpha_x|x\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe memory requirement grows rapidly with \\(n\\). Simulators may use other methods, such as tensor networks or stabilizer simulation, for circuits with special structure.\n\n## 14.3 Hardware execution\n\nOn quantum hardware, the abstract gates are translated into calibrated physical pulses. The result is affected by:\n\n- State-preparation error.\n- Gate error.\n- Decoherence.\n- Crosstalk.\n- Readout error.\n- Limited qubit connectivity.\n\nThe output is therefore a noisy sample distribution rather than a perfect mathematical result.\n\n## 14.4 Shots and counts\n\nA circuit is usually executed many times. Each execution is a shot. Suppose a Bell circuit is run 1,000 times and gives\n\n```text\n00 : 506\n11 : 482\n01 : 7\n10 : 5\n```\n\nThe ideal circuit predicts 00 and 11 with probability \\(1/2\\) each. The small counts of 01 and 10 may be caused by hardware noise or imperfect simulation settings.\n\nThe estimated probability of 00 is\n\n\\[\n\\hat P(00)=\\frac{506}{1000}=0.506.\n\\]\n\n## 14.5 Compilation and transpilation\n\nA compiler transforms a high-level circuit into operations supported by a target backend. It may:\n\n- Decompose gates into native gates.\n- Optimize consecutive operations.\n- Cancel inverse gates.\n- Schedule gates into moments.\n- Insert SWAP gates for routing.\n- Assign logical qubits to physical qubits.\n\nCompilation can reduce or increase circuit depth depending on the target hardware and optimization settings.\n\n## 14.6 Circuit execution workflow\n\nA practical workflow is:\n\n```text\nhigh-level circuit\n        ↓\ntranspile / compile\n        ↓\nbackend selection\n        ↓\nrun many shots\n        ↓\nmeasurement counts\n        ↓\nprobability estimates\n        ↓\nclassical analysis\n```\n\nQuantum computing is therefore usually hybrid. A classical computer constructs the circuit, sends it to a simulator or quantum processor, receives measurement results, and interprets them.\n\n---\n\n# 15. Worked examples\n\n## Example 7.1: One-qubit circuit\n\nAnalyze\n\n```text\nq: ──H──M──\n```\n\nAssume the initial state is \\(|0\\rangle\\). After the Hadamard gate,\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle\\xrightarrow{H}\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\n## Example 7.2: Reversible circuit\n\nAnalyze\n\n```text\nq: ──H──H──M──\n```\n\nThe first gate creates \\(|+\\rangle\\), and the second reverses it:\n\n\\[\nH^2|0\\rangle=|0\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe final measurement returns 0 with probability 1.\n\n## Example 7.3: Phase-sensitive circuit\n\nAnalyze\n\n```text\nq: ──H──Z──H──M──\n```\n\nThe state sequence is\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle\n\\xrightarrow{H}\n|+\\rangle\n\\xrightarrow{Z}\n|-\\rangle\n\\xrightarrow{H}\n|1\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThe final measurement returns 1 with probability 1.\n\n## Example 7.4: Bell-state circuit\n\nAnalyze\n\n```text\nq0: ──H────●──M──\n           │\nq1: ───────X──M──\n```\n\nStarting from \\(|00\\rangle\\),\n\n\\[\n|00\\rangle\n\\xrightarrow{H\\otimes I}\n\\frac{|00\\rangle+|10\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{CNOT}}\n\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThe outcomes are\n\n\\[\nP(00)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(11)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\n## Example 7.5: Circuit depth\n\nConsider\n\n```text\nq0: ──H────●────H──M──\n           │\nq1: ──X────X────Z──M──\n```\n\nAssuming gates in the same column are executed in parallel:\n\n- Layer 1: H and X.\n- Layer 2: CNOT.\n- Layer 3: H and Z.\n- Layer 4: measurement.\n\nThe unitary gate depth is 3, excluding measurement. The total gate count is 5 before measurement.\n\n## Example 7.6: Measuring only one qubit\n\nConsider the Bell state\n\n\\[\n|\\Phi^+\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nIf only qubit 0 is measured, then\n\n\\[\nP(q_0=0)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(q_0=1)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nIf the result is 0, the second qubit is conditionally in \\(|0\\rangle\\). If the result is 1, it is conditionally in \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\n---\n\n# 16. Common misconceptions\n\n## Misconception 1: A circuit diagram is only a picture\n\nA circuit diagram is also a precise mathematical specification of an ordered sequence of operations.\n\n## Misconception 2: A wire is a classical electrical wire\n\nA quantum wire represents the path of a qubit through a circuit. It does not necessarily describe the physical hardware connection.\n\n## Misconception 3: Gates in the same visual column always run simultaneously\n\nThey may be parallel if they act on disjoint qubits and the hardware permits it. Scheduling constraints can change the physical execution.\n\n## Misconception 4: Circuit depth is the same as gate count\n\nGate count measures total operations. Depth measures sequential layers. Many gates can contribute to one layer if they are parallel.\n\n## Misconception 5: Measurement can be placed anywhere without changing the computation\n\nEarly measurement can collapse a state and destroy coherence. Moving measurement changes the circuit unless the operations are known to commute with the measurement.\n\n## Misconception 6: A simulator and a real quantum processor produce identical results\n\nA simulator may model ideal operations, while real hardware includes noise, calibration errors, and readout errors.\n\n## Misconception 7: One circuit run is enough to determine all probabilities\n\nOne run produces one sample. Many shots are needed to estimate a probability distribution.\n\n---\n\n# 17. Chapter summary\n\nA quantum circuit is an ordered sequence of quantum gates and measurement operations acting on qubits. Qubit wires show the path of quantum information, and time flows from left to right.\n\nSingle-qubit gates are drawn as boxes on individual wires. Multi-qubit gates connect wires, with CNOT being a central example. Measurement converts quantum states into classical bits and generally changes the measured state.\n\nA sequence of gates \\(U_1,U_2,\\ldots,U_k\\) has total operation\n\n\\[\nU=U_k\\cdots U_2U_1.\n\\]\n\nGate order matters because quantum operations generally do not commute. Gates acting on independent qubits may sometimes be executed in parallel, which reduces circuit depth.\n\nCircuit width is the number of qubits. Gate count is the total number of gates. Circuit depth is the number of sequential gate layers. Depth matters because noise accumulates as a circuit runs for longer and uses more operations.\n\nCircuit execution involves initialization, compilation, backend selection, gate application, measurement, repeated shots, and classical analysis. Simulators provide ideal or modeled results, while hardware execution includes noise and readout errors.\n\nThe general workflow is\n\n\\[\n\\text{initialize}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{apply gates}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{measure}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{repeat}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{analyze counts}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n# 18. Formula sheet\n\nTotal unitary of a gate sequence:\n\n\\[\nU_{\\text{total}}=U_kU_{k-1}\\cdots U_1.\n\\]\n\nState evolution:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_k\\rangle=U_k\\cdots U_2U_1|\\psi_0\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nGeneral \\(n\\)-qubit state:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_{x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}\\alpha_x|x\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nMeasurement probability:\n\n\\[\nP(x)=|\\alpha_x|^2.\n\\]\n\nTensor-product operation:\n\n\\[\n(U\\otimes V)|\\psi\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nCNOT action:\n\n\\[\n|a,b\\rangle\\mapsto|a,a\\oplus b\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nCircuit width:\n\n\\[\n\\text{width}=\\text{number of qubits}.\n\\]\n\nCircuit depth:\n\n\\[\n\\text{depth}=\\text{number of sequential executable gate layers}.\n\\]\n\nEstimated probability from counts:\n\n\\[\n\\hat P(x)=\\frac{N_x}{N},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(N_x\\) is the number of times outcome \\(x\\) appears in \\(N\\) shots.\n\n---\n\n# 19. Review questions and exercises\n\n## Conceptual questions\n\n1. What is a quantum circuit?\n2. What do horizontal wires represent?\n3. In which direction does time normally flow in a circuit diagram?\n4. What is the difference between a quantum wire and a classical wire?\n5. Why does the order of gates matter?\n6. What is a circuit moment?\n7. What is circuit width?\n8. What is circuit depth?\n9. Why is circuit depth important on noisy hardware?\n10. What does measurement do in a circuit?\n11. What is the difference between a simulator and a quantum processor?\n12. Why are circuits executed for many shots?\n\n## Numerical exercises\n\n1. Calculate the final state of\n   ```text\n   q: ──H──H──M──\n   ```\n   when the initial state is \\(|0\\rangle\\).\n\n2. Calculate the final state of\n   ```text\n   q: ──H──Z──H──M──\n   ```\n   when the initial state is \\(|0\\rangle\\).\n\n3. Calculate the output probabilities of\n   ```text\n   q: ──H──M──\n   ```\n   when the initial state is \\(|1\\rangle\\).\n\n4. Analyze the Bell-state circuit and calculate the probabilities of all two-bit outcomes.\n\n5. Determine the gate count and depth of\n   ```text\n   q0: ──H────●────X──\n              │\n   q1: ──Z────X────H──\n   ```\n\n6. Calculate the probability estimate if outcome 101 appears 723 times in 1,000 shots.\n\n7. For the state\n   \\[\n   |\\psi\\rangle=\\frac12(|00\\rangle+|01\\rangle+|10\\rangle+|11\\rangle),\n   \\]\n   calculate the probability that the first qubit is 1.\n\n8. Explain why adding a SWAP gate may increase physical circuit depth even if it does not change the logical algorithm.\n\n## Circuit drawing exercises\n\nDraw circuits for:\n\n1. Creating \\(|+\\rangle\\) from \\(|0\\rangle\\).\n2. Applying X, then H, then measuring a qubit.\n3. Creating a Bell state.\n4. Measuring only the first qubit of a two-qubit circuit.\n5. Applying H and X in parallel to two separate qubits.\n\n## Programming activity\n\nUsing Qiskit, Cirq, PennyLane, or another quantum framework:\n\n1. Build the circuit \\(H\\)-\\(H\\) and verify the output counts.\n2. Build the circuit \\(H\\)-\\(Z\\)-\\(H\\) and verify the output counts.\n3. Build a Bell-state circuit using H and CNOT.\n4. Execute the Bell circuit for 100, 1,000, and 10,000 shots.\n5. Compare ideal simulator counts with noisy simulator counts if available.\n6. Inspect the compiled circuit and compare its logical and physical depth.\n\n---\n\n# 20. Glossary\n\n**Barrier:** A circuit-organization marker that separates groups of operations.\n\n**Circuit depth:** The number of sequential layers of gates in a circuit.\n\n**Circuit execution:** The process of compiling, running, measuring, and analyzing a quantum circuit.\n\n**Circuit width:** The number of qubits used by a circuit.\n\n**Classical wire:** A line that carries a classical measurement result.\n\n**Moment:** A time layer in which compatible gates may be executed in parallel.\n\n**Quantum circuit:** An ordered arrangement of qubits, quantum gates, and measurements.\n\n**Quantum wire:** A diagrammatic path representing the evolution of a qubit.\n\n**Shot:** One execution of a circuit followed by measurement.\n\n**Transpilation:** Translation and optimization of a circuit for a specific quantum backend.\n\n---\n\n# References\n\n[1]: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information \"IBM Quantum Learning: Basics of Quantum Information\"\n\n[2]: https://qiskit.qotlabs.org/learning/courses/quantum-computing-in-practice \"IBM Quantum Learning: Quantum Computing in Practice\"\n\n[3]: https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.68.1049 \"Quantum Computation and Quantum Information by Michael A. Nielsen and Isaac L. Chuang\"\n\n[4]: https://theory.caltech.edu/~preskill/ph229/ \"John Preskill: Lecture Notes for Physics 229, Quantum Information and Computation\"\n\n[5]: https://www.nist.gov/quantum-information-science \"National Institute of Standards and Technology: Quantum Information Science\"\n\n## Key formulas: derivation and interpretation\n\n### Sequential gate application\n\nFor gates \\(U_1,U_2,\\ldots,U_k\\) applied in that order:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi_{\\text{out}}\\rangle=U_kU_{k-1}\\cdots U_2U_1|\\psi_{\\text{in}}\\rangle.\n\\]\n\n**Derivation:** The first gate produces \\(|\\psi_1\\rangle=U_1|\\psi_{\\text{in}}\\rangle\\). The second produces \\(|\\psi_2\\rangle=U_2|\\psi_1\\rangle=U_2U_1|\\psi_{\\text{in}}\\rangle\\). Continuing this process gives the full product. The rightmost matrix acts first.\n\n### Circuit depth\n\nCircuit depth is the number of sequential layers required when gates acting on different qubits can be placed in the same layer. Lower depth generally reduces exposure to noise, although the exact error depends on hardware and gate quality.\n\n### How the Chapter 7 formulas are obtained\n\n#### Deriving the gate-sequence formula\n\n1. Start with \\(|\\psi_{in}\\rangle\\).\n2. After the first gate, \\(|\\psi_1\\rangle=U_1|\\psi_{in}\\rangle\\).\n3. After the second gate, \\(|\\psi_2\\rangle=U_2U_1|\\psi_{in}\\rangle\\).\n4. Continue applying gates from left to right in time.\n5. After k gates, the last-applied matrix is on the left: \\(|\\psi_{out}\\rangle=U_k\\cdots U_2U_1|\\psi_{in}\\rangle\\).\n6. The rightmost operation acts first because matrix multiplication represents function composition.\n\n**Variables:** \\(|\\psi_{in}\\rangle\\,|\\psi_{out}\\rangle\\) are input and output states; \\(U_1,\\ldots,U_k\\) are gates; k is the number of sequential gates. Circuit depth counts parallel layers, not necessarily total gates.\n\n## Chapter assessment\n\n### One-mark questions and answers\n\n1. **Question:** What is a quantum circuit?\n   **Answer:** An ordered sequence of quantum operations on qubits.\n\n2. **Question:** What do wires represent?\n   **Answer:** Qubit timelines.\n\n3. **Question:** What is circuit depth?\n   **Answer:** The number of sequential gate layers.\n\n4. **Question:** Why is gate order important?\n   **Answer:** Quantum gates generally do not commute.\n\n5. **Question:** What is a shot?\n   **Answer:** One circuit execution followed by measurement.\n\n### Chapter quiz\n\n1. What is a quantum circuit?\n\n2. What do wires represent?\n\n3. What is circuit depth?\n\n4. Why is gate order important?\n\n5. What is a shot?\n\n### Quiz answer key\n\n1. **An ordered sequence of quantum operations on qubits.**\n\n2. **Qubit timelines.**\n\n3. **The number of sequential gate layers.**\n\n4. **Quantum gates generally do not commute.**\n\n5. **One circuit execution followed by measurement.**\n\n---\n"
} as const;


