export const chapter11 = {
  id: "m2-ch11",
  moduleId: "module-2",
  title: "Quantum Computing Platforms",
  description: "Quantum simulators, quantum hardware, NISQ systems, circuit compilation, execution workflows, and platform evaluation.",
  content: "# Chapter 11: Quantum Computing Platforms\n\n## 11.1 Chapter overview\n\nA **quantum computing platform** is the combination of software, mathematical models, control systems, and physical hardware used to create and execute quantum circuits. Different platforms provide different ways to experiment with quantum algorithms:\n\n- **Quantum simulators** imitate quantum circuits using classical computers.\n- **Quantum hardware** performs quantum operations on physical quantum systems.\n- **Gate-based quantum computers** execute algorithms as sequences of gates and measurements.\n- **Noisy intermediate-scale quantum (NISQ) systems** are current or near-term quantum processors with useful quantum control but limited qubit counts and imperfect operations.\n\nA quantum circuit written at the algorithm level may be executed in several ways. It can be simulated ideally using a state-vector simulator, sampled using a shot-based simulator, tested with a noise model, or sent to a real quantum processor. The mathematical circuit may be the same, but the execution environment changes the cost, speed, accuracy, and interpretation of the results.\n\nA practical workflow is\n\n\\[\n\\text{design circuit}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{simulate ideally}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{test with noise}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{compile for hardware}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{execute and analyze results}.\n\\]\n\nUnderstanding platforms is important because a successful algorithm requires more than correct mathematics. It must also fit the available gate set, qubit connectivity, coherence time, measurement system, and error budget.\n\n---\n\n## 11.2 Learning objectives\n\nAfter studying this chapter, learners should be able to:\n\n1. Define a quantum computing platform.\n2. Explain the purpose and limitations of quantum simulators.\n3. Distinguish state-vector, shot-based, stabilizer, and tensor-network simulation.\n4. Describe the basic components of a quantum hardware system.\n5. Explain the gate-based quantum computing model.\n6. Define NISQ systems and their main limitations.\n7. Compare ideal simulation, noisy simulation, and hardware execution.\n8. Explain compilation, transpilation, and native gate sets.\n9. Build and interpret simple circuits on a simulator.\n10. Use shots and counts to estimate measurement probabilities.\n11. Identify important hardware metrics such as fidelity, coherence time, and connectivity.\n12. Choose an appropriate platform for an introductory quantum experiment.\n\n---\n\n# 12. What is a quantum computing platform?\n\n## 12.1 Definition\n\nA quantum computing platform is an environment in which quantum circuits can be designed, transformed, executed, and analyzed. It usually contains:\n\n1. A circuit description language or programming interface.\n2. A compiler or transpiler.\n3. A simulator, quantum processor, or both.\n4. A measurement and result-collection system.\n5. Classical software for post-processing and visualization.\n\nA platform may be entirely software-based, entirely hardware-based, or a combination of both.\n\n## 12.2 Layers of a platform\n\nQuantum platforms can be understood as a stack:\n\n```text\napplication / algorithm\n          ↓\nquantum circuit\n          ↓\ncompiler and transpiler\n          ↓\nnative gate sequence\n          ↓\ncontrol pulses and hardware instructions\n          ↓\nphysical qubits\n          ↓\nmeasurement and classical results\n```\n\nAt the top, a programmer may write a circuit using H, X, CNOT, and measurement. At the bottom, the device may implement those operations using microwave pulses, laser pulses, optical elements, or other physical controls.\n\n## 12.3 Logical and physical descriptions\n\nA **logical circuit** describes the algorithm independently of a particular processor. A **physical circuit** is the compiled version that uses the target hardware's native operations and connectivity.\n\nFor example, a logical circuit may contain a CNOT between two qubits that are not physically adjacent. The compiler may insert SWAP operations so that the interaction can be implemented. The physical circuit then has more gates and greater depth.\n\n## 12.4 Why platforms differ\n\nPlatforms differ in:\n\n- Number of available qubits.\n- Native gate set.\n- Qubit connectivity.\n- Gate fidelity.\n- Measurement fidelity.\n- Coherence time.\n- Supported noise models.\n- Compilation tools.\n- Access method and queue time.\n- Cost of computation.\n\nA circuit that works well on one platform may perform poorly on another. Platform selection is therefore part of quantum algorithm engineering.\n\n---\n\n# 13. Quantum simulators\n\n## 13.1 Definition\n\nA **quantum simulator** is a classical program that calculates or approximates the behavior of a quantum circuit. It does not contain physical quantum bits. Instead, it stores mathematical information about quantum states and applies simulated operations.\n\nSimulators are useful for:\n\n- Learning quantum mechanics and circuit notation.\n- Debugging algorithms.\n- Verifying small circuits.\n- Comparing theoretical and experimental results.\n- Testing noise models.\n- Exploring circuits before using hardware.\n- Generating training data for quantum machine learning.\n\n## 13.2 State-vector simulation\n\nAn ideal pure \\(n\\)-qubit state is represented by\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_{x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}\\alpha_x|x\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(2^n\\) complex amplitudes. A state-vector simulator stores these amplitudes and applies matrix operations corresponding to gates.\n\nFor example, applying a one-qubit gate \\(U\\) to qubit \\(j\\) is represented by a large \\(2^n\\times2^n\\) operator acting on the full state vector. Software uses optimized tensor operations rather than constructing every large matrix explicitly.\n\nState-vector simulation provides detailed information about the complete ideal state, including amplitudes and phases. However, its memory requirement grows exponentially with the number of qubits.\n\n## 13.3 Memory scaling\n\nThe number of amplitudes for \\(n\\) qubits is\n\n\\[\n2^n.\n\\]\n\nIf each complex amplitude uses 16 bytes in double precision, the approximate memory requirement is\n\n\\[\nM(n)\\approx16\\cdot2^n\\text{ bytes}.\n\\]\n\nFor example:\n\n| Qubits | Amplitudes | Approximate state-vector memory |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 10 | 1,024 | 16 KB |\n| 20 | 1,048,576 | 16 MB |\n| 30 | 1,073,741,824 | 16 GB |\n| 40 | 1,099,511,627,776 | 16 TB |\n\nThe exact practical limit depends on hardware, compression, parallelization, and simulator implementation. The exponential scaling is the central challenge.\n\n## 13.4 Shot-based simulation\n\nA shot-based simulator samples measurement outcomes from the probability distribution rather than returning the full state vector. If a circuit produces probabilities \\(P(x)\\), each shot returns one string \\(x\\).\n\nFor \\(N\\) shots, the estimated probability is\n\n\\[\n\\hat P(x)=\\frac{N_x}{N},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(N_x\\) is the number of times outcome \\(x\\) appears.\n\nShot-based simulation resembles hardware execution and is useful for testing result-processing code.\n\n## 13.5 Stabilizer simulation\n\nSome circuits can be simulated efficiently using the stabilizer formalism. Stabilizer circuits are generated primarily by Clifford operations such as:\n\n- H.\n- S.\n- CNOT.\n- Pauli X, Y, and Z.\n- Preparation and measurement in compatible bases.\n\nA stabilizer simulator does not store every amplitude explicitly. It stores a compact description of the stabilizer group. This allows some circuits with many qubits to be simulated efficiently.\n\nThe efficiency does not apply to arbitrary circuits. Adding non-Clifford gates such as T can make the simulation more difficult.\n\n## 13.6 Tensor-network simulation\n\nA tensor-network simulator represents a quantum state or circuit as connected tensors. It can be efficient when the circuit has limited entanglement, low depth, or favorable geometry.\n\nTensor-network methods are useful for:\n\n- One-dimensional circuits.\n- Shallow circuits.\n- Systems with low entanglement.\n- Certain structured algorithms.\n\nHighly entangled circuits can cause the tensor network to grow rapidly and become expensive to contract.\n\n## 13.7 Noisy simulation\n\nA noisy simulator applies a mathematical error model in addition to the intended gates. It can model:\n\n- Bit-flip errors.\n- Phase-flip errors.\n- Depolarizing errors.\n- Readout errors.\n- Amplitude damping.\n- Phase damping.\n- Thermal relaxation.\n- Gate-dependent noise.\n\nA noisy simulator is useful for estimating how an algorithm may behave on hardware, but its predictions depend on how accurately the noise model represents the device.\n\n## 13.8 Simulator strengths and limitations\n\n### Strengths\n\n- Repeatable and controllable.\n- Usually easier to access than hardware.\n- Can expose the exact ideal state.\n- Useful for debugging.\n- Allows controlled noise experiments.\n- No queue time or hardware calibration drift.\n\n### Limitations\n\n- Large arbitrary circuits become exponentially expensive.\n- Noise models may not match hardware.\n- Simulation does not reproduce every hardware constraint.\n- A circuit that works ideally may fail on a real device.\n- Simulator performance can hide compilation and connectivity costs.\n\n---\n\n# 14. Quantum hardware: basic overview\n\n## 14.1 Physical quantum systems\n\nA quantum computer uses controllable physical systems as qubits. Common technology families include:\n\n- Superconducting circuits.\n- Trapped ions.\n- Neutral atoms.\n- Photonic systems.\n- Semiconductor spin qubits.\n- Quantum dots.\n- Topological approaches under research.\n\nEach platform has different methods for initialization, gate control, interaction, and measurement.\n\n## 14.2 Basic hardware components\n\nA gate-based quantum processor typically requires:\n\n1. **Quantum processing unit (QPU):** The physical device containing qubits.\n2. **Control electronics:** Hardware that generates pulses or fields.\n3. **Readout system:** Hardware that measures qubit states.\n4. **Calibration system:** Software and instruments that characterize operations.\n5. **Cryogenic or environmental system:** Infrastructure that maintains the required physical conditions.\n6. **Classical controller:** A computer that schedules operations and processes results.\n\n## 14.3 Initialization\n\nA circuit generally begins by preparing qubits in a known state, often \\(|0\\rangle\\). Initialization may involve cooling, optical pumping, reset pulses, measurement-based reset, or another physical procedure.\n\nInitialization is imperfect. The probability of preparing the intended state is described by state-preparation fidelity or error rate.\n\n## 14.4 Gate control\n\nA logical gate is implemented using a physical control signal. Examples include:\n\n- Microwave pulses for superconducting qubits.\n- Laser pulses for trapped ions.\n- Laser or optical tweezer operations for neutral atoms.\n- Beam splitters and phase shifters for photonic qubits.\n- Voltage and magnetic control for semiconductor spin qubits.\n\nThe pulse sequence is designed to produce a particular unitary transformation while minimizing leakage and unwanted interactions.\n\n## 14.5 Qubit connectivity\n\nA hardware device may not allow every physical qubit to interact directly. A connectivity graph describes which pairs can perform native two-qubit gates.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nq0 ── q1 ── q2 ── q3\n```\n\nIf an algorithm requires interaction between q0 and q3, the compiler may insert SWAP gates or use a routing strategy.\n\nConnectivity affects:\n\n- Circuit depth.\n- Number of two-qubit gates.\n- Error accumulation.\n- Compilation quality.\n- Algorithm performance.\n\n## 14.6 Measurement and readout\n\nMeasurement converts a physical qubit state into a classical result. Readout is imperfect, so a prepared 0 may occasionally be reported as 1 and vice versa.\n\nA readout confusion matrix can be written as\n\n\\[\nM=\n\\begin{bmatrix}\nP(0\\mid0)&P(0\\mid1)\\\\\nP(1\\mid0)&P(1\\mid1)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThe columns describe the prepared state and the rows describe the reported result. Readout calibration can estimate this matrix and support measurement-error mitigation.\n\n## 14.7 Environmental isolation\n\nQuantum states are sensitive to unwanted interactions with the environment. Hardware may require:\n\n- Very low temperatures.\n- Vacuum systems.\n- Electromagnetic shielding.\n- Vibration isolation.\n- Laser stabilization.\n- Low-noise electronics.\n\nThe engineering challenge is to control the qubits strongly enough to perform gates while isolating them enough to preserve coherence.\n\n---\n\n# 15. Gate-based quantum computers\n\n## 15.1 Definition\n\nA **gate-based quantum computer** performs computation by applying a sequence of quantum gates to qubits and measuring the final state. It is the circuit model studied throughout this module.\n\nA typical gate-based computation is\n\n\\[\n|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}\n\\xrightarrow{U_1}\n\\xrightarrow{U_2}\n\\cdots\n\\xrightarrow{U_k}\n\\text{measurement}.\n\\]\n\nThe gates may include one-qubit rotations, phase gates, entangling gates, and controlled operations.\n\n## 15.2 Universal gate sets\n\nA gate set is **universal** if its gates can approximate any unitary operation to arbitrary accuracy, given enough gates. Common universal constructions combine:\n\n- A collection of Clifford gates.\n- At least one suitable non-Clifford gate, such as T.\n- An entangling two-qubit gate, such as CNOT or CZ.\n\nThe exact gate set depends on the hardware and compiler.\n\n## 15.3 Native gates\n\nA **native gate** is an operation directly supported by the hardware or its low-level control system. A high-level gate may be decomposed into native gates.\n\nFor example, if a processor supports rotations and CZ natively, a CNOT may be compiled using\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{CNOT}=(I\\otimes H)\\operatorname{CZ}(I\\otimes H).\n\\]\n\nThe decomposition changes the physical gate count and depth.\n\n## 15.4 Compilation and transpilation\n\nCompilation translates an abstract circuit into a form suitable for a target device. It may:\n\n- Decompose unsupported gates.\n- Optimize redundant operations.\n- Cancel adjacent inverse gates.\n- Map logical qubits to physical qubits.\n- Insert routing gates.\n- Schedule operations.\n- Respect timing and calibration constraints.\n\nA compiler must balance circuit depth, gate fidelity, routing overhead, and execution time.\n\n## 15.5 Hardware-aware circuits\n\nA hardware-aware algorithm considers the device while designing the circuit. It may:\n\n- Use the native entangling gate.\n- Place strongly interacting logical qubits on connected physical qubits.\n- Avoid unnecessary basis changes.\n- Reduce two-qubit gate count.\n- Use shallow variational ansätze.\n- Choose measurement groupings carefully.\n\nHardware-aware design is especially important for NISQ systems.\n\n---\n\n# 16. Noisy intermediate-scale quantum systems\n\n## 16.1 Meaning of NISQ\n\n**NISQ** stands for **noisy intermediate-scale quantum**. The term describes quantum processors that have enough qubits and control capability to run nontrivial circuits, but are not yet fully fault-tolerant.\n\nThe three words emphasize:\n\n- **Noisy:** Gates, measurements, initialization, and memory are imperfect.\n- **Intermediate-scale:** The systems are larger than small laboratory demonstrations but not necessarily large enough for general fault-tolerant computation.\n- **Quantum:** The processors can create and manipulate superposition, interference, and entanglement.\n\nThe boundaries of the NISQ era are not defined by one exact qubit count. Performance depends on qubit quality, connectivity, control, error rates, and circuit depth.\n\n## 16.2 Sources of noise\n\nImportant noise sources include:\n\n- State-preparation error.\n- Single-qubit gate error.\n- Two-qubit gate error.\n- Readout error.\n- Decoherence.\n- Crosstalk.\n- Leakage outside the computational subspace.\n- Frequency drift and calibration error.\n- Thermal or environmental fluctuations.\n\nTwo-qubit gates are often more error-prone than single-qubit gates, making entangling circuit depth a key concern.\n\n## 16.3 Coherence times\n\nA qubit can lose information through relaxation and dephasing. Two commonly discussed times are:\n\n- \\(T_1\\): energy-relaxation time.\n- \\(T_2\\): phase-coherence time.\n\nA rough operating requirement is that a circuit should finish before noise significantly destroys the useful state. Gate duration, circuit depth, and scheduling all influence this relationship.\n\n## 16.4 Fidelity and error rate\n\nGate fidelity measures how closely an implemented operation matches its ideal operation. An error rate is a measure of deviation from ideal behavior.\n\nIf a circuit contains many noisy operations, the total success probability generally decreases. A simplified independent-error model might estimate\n\n\\[\nP_{\\text{success}}\\approx(1-p)^G,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p\\) is an average error rate per gate and \\(G\\) is the number of gates. Real devices have correlated and gate-dependent errors, so this formula is only an intuition-building approximation.\n\n## 16.5 NISQ algorithm families\n\nNISQ research commonly studies hybrid algorithms, including:\n\n- Variational quantum eigensolver (VQE).\n- Quantum approximate optimization algorithm (QAOA).\n- Variational quantum classifiers.\n- Quantum kernel methods.\n- Error mitigation methods.\n- Short-depth quantum simulation.\n\nThese algorithms use a classical optimizer to update parameters in a quantum circuit based on measured results.\n\n## 16.6 Error mitigation versus error correction\n\n**Quantum error correction** encodes logical information into many physical qubits and actively protects it against errors. It requires substantial hardware overhead.\n\n**Error mitigation** does not fully correct errors during the computation. Instead, it estimates or reduces their effect after collecting results. Examples include:\n\n- Readout-error mitigation.\n- Zero-noise extrapolation.\n- Probabilistic error cancellation.\n- Symmetry verification.\n\nError mitigation is useful for NISQ experiments but does not provide the same long-term protection as fault-tolerant error correction.\n\n## 16.7 NISQ limitations\n\nNISQ systems are limited by:\n\n- Short effective circuit depth.\n- Imperfect two-qubit gates.\n- Restricted connectivity.\n- Measurement overhead.\n- Calibration drift.\n- Limited error correction.\n- Difficult scaling.\n- Classical optimization challenges.\n\nA NISQ result should therefore be compared against a strong classical baseline and evaluated with statistical care.\n\n---\n\n# 17. Quantum circuits on simulators\n\n## 17.1 A first simulator circuit\n\nConsider the circuit\n\n```text\nq: ──H──M──\n```\n\nStarting from \\(|0\\rangle\\), the state becomes\n\n\\[\nH|0\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThe ideal probabilities are\n\n\\[\nP(0)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(1)=\\frac12.\n\\]\n\nA shot-based simulator may return approximate counts such as\n\n```text\n0 : 503\n1 : 497\n```\n\nfor 1,000 shots.\n\n## 17.2 Simulating a Bell circuit\n\nThe H+CNOT circuit is\n\n```text\nq0: ──H────●──M──\n           │\nq1: ───────X──M──\n```\n\nThe ideal final state is\n\n\\[\n|\\Phi^+\\rangle=\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nThe ideal distribution is\n\n\\[\nP(00)=\\frac12,\n\\qquad\nP(11)=\\frac12,\n\\]\n\nwith zero probability for 01 and 10.\n\nA 1,000-shot ideal simulation may produce approximately\n\n```text\n00 : 497\n11 : 503\n```\n\n## 17.3 Simulator pseudocode\n\nThe following pseudocode illustrates the common workflow:\n\n```text\ncreate a quantum circuit with 2 qubits\napply H to qubit 0\napply CNOT from qubit 0 to qubit 1\nmeasure both qubits\nrun the circuit for 1000 shots\ncollect output counts\nplot or analyze the distribution\n```\n\nThe exact syntax differs across software frameworks, but the conceptual steps are the same.\n\n## 17.4 State-vector versus shot execution\n\nA state-vector simulator may return\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n\\]\n\ndirectly. A shot-based simulator returns samples such as 00 and 11. The state-vector reveals amplitudes and phase; the shot-based result resembles what a hardware device provides.\n\nA complete development workflow often uses both:\n\n1. State-vector simulation to verify the mathematics.\n2. Shot-based simulation to test result processing.\n3. Noisy simulation to estimate robustness.\n4. Hardware execution to assess real behavior.\n\n## 17.5 Noisy Bell-circuit simulation\n\nSuppose a noise model causes a small probability of bit-flip errors. The simulated output might become\n\n```text\n00 : 472\n01 : 21\n10 : 18\n11 : 489\n```\n\nThe ideal correlations remain visible, but forbidden outcomes appear. A useful analysis should report:\n\n- Number of shots.\n- Noise model.\n- Joint probabilities.\n- Marginal probabilities.\n- Correlation values.\n- Any mitigation or post-processing.\n\n## 17.6 Parameterized circuits\n\nA parameterized circuit contains gates whose angles depend on classical parameters:\n\n```text\nq0: ──Ry(θ)────●──M──\n               │\nq1: ───────────X──M──\n```\n\nThe circuit is executed for different values of \\(\\theta\\). A classical optimizer may use the measured expectation value to update \\(\\theta\\).\n\nParameterized circuits are central to variational algorithms and hybrid quantum-classical computing.\n\n## 17.7 Comparing simulator and hardware results\n\nA meaningful comparison should keep the following consistent:\n\n- Circuit definition.\n- Qubit order.\n- Gate decomposition.\n- Number of shots.\n- Measurement basis.\n- Initial state.\n- Noise assumptions.\n- Classical post-processing.\n\nA difference between simulator and hardware results may be caused by noise, compilation, qubit mapping, readout conventions, or an implementation bug.\n\n---\n\n# 18. Choosing a platform\n\n## 18.1 Use an ideal simulator when\n\nChoose an ideal simulator when you want to:\n\n- Learn circuit behavior.\n- Verify algebraic derivations.\n- Inspect state vectors.\n- Debug gate order.\n- Test small algorithms.\n- Confirm expected probabilities.\n\n## 18.2 Use a noisy simulator when\n\nChoose a noisy simulator when you want to:\n\n- Estimate the effect of errors.\n- Test error mitigation.\n- Compare circuit depths.\n- Study robustness.\n- Develop hardware-aware algorithms without waiting for a device.\n\n## 18.3 Use real hardware when\n\nUse hardware when you need to:\n\n- Validate physical execution.\n- Study device-specific noise.\n- Test compilation and connectivity.\n- Measure calibration-dependent behavior.\n- Demonstrate an experiment beyond an ideal model.\n\nHardware results should be treated as experimental data, not automatically as exact mathematical truth.\n\n## 18.4 Platform-selection checklist\n\nBefore running a circuit, ask:\n\n1. How many qubits does the circuit require?\n2. What two-qubit interactions are required?\n3. Is the circuit shallow enough for the expected noise level?\n4. What gates are native to the target platform?\n5. Does the device support the required measurement basis?\n6. How many shots are needed?\n7. Is the simulator memory requirement manageable?\n8. What classical baseline should be used?\n9. How will errors and confidence intervals be reported?\n10. Is the objective learning, verification, noise analysis, or hardware demonstration?\n\n---\n\n# 19. Worked examples\n\n## Example 11.1: Ideal Hadamard simulation\n\nA one-qubit circuit starts in \\(|0\\rangle\\) and applies H. The final state is\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(N=2,000\\) shots, an ideal simulator may produce counts near\n\n\\[\nN_0\\approx1000,\n\\qquad\nN_1\\approx1000.\n\\]\n\nThe expected standard deviation of the count for a probability \\(p\\) is approximately\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_N=\\sqrt{Np(1-p)}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(p=1/2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_N=\\sqrt{2000\\cdot\\frac12\\cdot\\frac12}\\approx22.36.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, counts such as 978 and 1,022 are compatible with ordinary sampling variation.\n\n## Example 11.2: Bell circuit on an ideal simulator\n\nThe circuit\n\n```text\nq0: ──H────●──M──\n           │\nq1: ───────X──M──\n```\n\nproduces \\(|\\Phi^+\\rangle\\). For 4,000 shots, the ideal expected counts are approximately\n\n\\[\nN_{00}=2000,\n\\qquad\nN_{11}=2000.\n\\]\n\nOutcomes 01 and 10 should have zero ideal probability.\n\n## Example 11.3: Estimating an error rate\n\nSuppose a Bell-circuit experiment produces 100 counts of 01 or 10 out of 2,000 shots. An empirical correlation-error rate is\n\n\\[\n\\hat e=\\frac{100}{2000}=0.05.\n\\]\n\nThis does not identify the cause by itself. The error may arise from gate errors, readout errors, initialization, decoherence, or a combination.\n\n## Example 11.4: Hardware-aware depth\n\nA logical circuit requires four CNOT gates. On a platform with direct connectivity, it may execute at depth 4. On a linearly connected device, routing SWAP gates may be inserted, increasing the depth to 7.\n\nIf the average two-qubit error rate is \\(p=0.01\\), a simple independent-error estimate gives\n\n\\[\n(1-p)^4\\approx0.9606,\n\\]\n\nfor the four-gate version, and\n\n\\[\n(1-p)^7\\approx0.9321,\n\\]\n\nfor the seven-gate version. This simplified calculation illustrates why routing overhead matters.\n\n## Example 11.5: Ideal, noisy, and hardware results\n\nSuppose a circuit has the following distributions:\n\n| Outcome | Ideal simulator | Noisy simulator | Hardware |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 00 | 0.500 | 0.475 | 0.462 |\n| 01 | 0.000 | 0.025 | 0.034 |\n| 10 | 0.000 | 0.024 | 0.041 |\n| 11 | 0.500 | 0.476 | 0.463 |\n\nThe ideal circuit predicts perfect correlation. The noisy simulator predicts correlation loss according to its model. Hardware has a similar but not identical pattern. The comparison is meaningful only if shot count, qubit ordering, circuit compilation, and measurement conventions are documented.\n\n---\n\n# 20. Common misconceptions\n\n## Misconception 1: A simulator is a quantum computer\n\nA simulator runs on classical hardware and calculates a model of quantum behavior. It does not provide the same scaling or physical noise as a QPU.\n\n## Misconception 2: More simulated qubits always means a more realistic experiment\n\nA large ideal simulation may ignore hardware connectivity, pulse errors, crosstalk, and readout noise. Realism depends on the model, not only the qubit count.\n\n## Misconception 3: NISQ means the device is useless\n\nNISQ systems can demonstrate quantum effects and support meaningful research, but algorithms must be designed around noise, depth, and measurement limitations.\n\n## Misconception 4: A high qubit count guarantees better performance\n\nUseful performance depends on qubit quality, connectivity, fidelity, coherence, calibration, and the algorithm's structure.\n\n## Misconception 5: Hardware and ideal-simulator results should be identical\n\nHardware results contain noise and statistical variation. Differences are expected and must be analyzed.\n\n## Misconception 6: Circuit depth is only a software statistic\n\nDepth relates to execution time, coherence loss, scheduling, and the number of opportunities for physical errors.\n\n## Misconception 7: A zero-probability ideal outcome can never appear on hardware\n\nNoise and readout errors can produce outcomes that have zero ideal probability.\n\n## Misconception 8: The first simulator that runs a circuit is the best platform\n\nThe appropriate simulator depends on circuit size, entanglement, noise requirements, output information, and available classical resources.\n\n---\n\n# 21. Chapter summary\n\nA quantum computing platform combines circuit software, compilers, simulators, control systems, quantum hardware, measurement, and classical analysis.\n\nQuantum simulators imitate circuits on classical computers. State-vector simulators store amplitudes, shot-based simulators sample outcomes, stabilizer simulators efficiently represent certain Clifford circuits, tensor-network methods exploit circuit structure, and noisy simulators model imperfect operations.\n\nQuantum hardware uses physical systems such as superconducting circuits, trapped ions, neutral atoms, photons, and semiconductor spins. A hardware platform must initialize qubits, apply controlled operations, preserve coherence, measure states, and communicate results to classical systems.\n\nGate-based quantum computers execute circuits as ordered sequences of gates and measurements. Compilers transform logical circuits into native operations and may add routing gates because of hardware connectivity.\n\nNISQ systems are noisy intermediate-scale processors. They can create and manipulate quantum states but are not fully fault-tolerant. Their performance is limited by gate errors, readout errors, decoherence, crosstalk, connectivity, and circuit depth.\n\nQuantum circuits should usually be tested through a progression:\n\n\\[\n\\text{ideal simulation}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{shot-based simulation}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{noisy simulation}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{hardware execution}.\n\\]\n\nA strong quantum-computing experiment reports the circuit, backend, compiler settings, shot count, measurement convention, noise assumptions, and classical comparison.\n\n---\n\n# 22. Formula sheet\n\nGeneral \\(n\\)-qubit state:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_{x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}\\alpha_x|x\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nNumber of amplitudes:\n\n\\[\n2^n.\n\\]\n\nState-vector memory estimate:\n\n\\[\nM(n)\\approx16\\cdot2^n\\text{ bytes}\n\\]\n\nfor 16-byte complex amplitudes.\n\nMeasurement probability:\n\n\\[\nP(x)=|\\alpha_x|^2.\n\\]\n\nEstimated probability from shots:\n\n\\[\n\\hat P(x)=\\frac{N_x}{N}.\n\\]\n\nApproximate binomial count deviation:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_N=\\sqrt{Np(1-p)}.\n\\]\n\nSimple independent gate-success estimate:\n\n\\[\nP_{\\text{success}}\\approx(1-p)^G.\n\\]\n\nReadout confusion matrix:\n\n\\[\nM=\n\\begin{bmatrix}\nP(0\\mid0)&P(0\\mid1)\\\\\nP(1\\mid0)&P(1\\mid1)\n\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nCNOT-CZ basis conversion:\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{CNOT}=(I\\otimes H)\\operatorname{CZ}(I\\otimes H).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n# 23. Review questions and exercises\n\n## Conceptual questions\n\n1. What is a quantum computing platform?\n2. What is the purpose of a quantum simulator?\n3. How does state-vector simulation differ from shot-based simulation?\n4. Why does state-vector memory scale exponentially?\n5. What are stabilizer and tensor-network simulators designed to exploit?\n6. What are the basic components of a quantum hardware platform?\n7. What is a native gate?\n8. Why does a compiler insert SWAP gates?\n9. What does NISQ mean?\n10. What are the main sources of noise in a NISQ processor?\n11. What is the difference between error mitigation and error correction?\n12. Why should circuits be tested on an ideal simulator before hardware?\n\n## Numerical exercises\n\n1. Calculate the number of amplitudes for 5, 10, and 20 qubits.\n2. Estimate state-vector memory for 25 qubits using 16 bytes per amplitude.\n3. A circuit produces outcome 101 in 734 of 2,000 shots. Calculate its estimated probability.\n4. For a fair binary outcome measured 1,600 times, estimate the standard deviation of the count.\n5. A circuit has 50 gates with average independent error rate 0.002. Estimate \\((1-p)^G\\).\n6. Calculate the ideal measurement probabilities of the H+CNOT Bell circuit.\n7. Explain how adding three routing gates affects a circuit with ten original two-qubit gates.\n8. Interpret a readout matrix in which \\(P(1\\mid0)=0.03\\) and \\(P(0\\mid1)=0.04\\).\n\n## Platform-comparison exercises\n\nFor each situation, choose an ideal simulator, noisy simulator, or hardware and explain why:\n\n1. Learning how H transforms \\(|0\\rangle\\).\n2. Debugging a 12-qubit algorithm's gate ordering.\n3. Testing a readout-error mitigation method.\n4. Studying whether a circuit survives a particular processor's connectivity.\n5. Demonstrating a real experimental Bell-state distribution.\n\n## Programming activity\n\nUsing Qiskit, Cirq, PennyLane, or another framework:\n\n1. Build and simulate a one-qubit H circuit.\n2. Build the H+CNOT Bell circuit.\n3. Run both state-vector and shot-based simulation.\n4. Add a bit-flip or depolarizing noise model.\n5. Compare ideal and noisy counts.\n6. Calculate marginal probabilities and correlation values.\n7. Inspect the compiled circuit for a selected backend.\n8. Compare circuit depth before and after transpilation.\n9. Run a small circuit on hardware if access is available.\n10. Document backend name, shots, gate set, qubit mapping, and result distribution.\n\n---\n\n# 24. Glossary\n\n**Backend:** The simulator or quantum processor on which a circuit is executed.\n\n**Compiler/transpiler:** Software that converts an abstract circuit into operations supported by a target platform.\n\n**Connectivity:** The set of qubit pairs that can directly interact on a device.\n\n**Fidelity:** A measure of how closely an implemented state or operation matches the ideal one.\n\n**Gate-based quantum computer:** A quantum computer that performs computation using sequences of quantum gates and measurements.\n\n**Native gate:** A gate directly supported by a hardware platform.\n\n**NISQ:** Noisy intermediate-scale quantum; a class of imperfect processors without full fault tolerance.\n\n**QPU:** Quantum processing unit containing the physical qubits and control interface.\n\n**Quantum simulator:** A classical program that models quantum circuits.\n\n**Shot:** One complete circuit execution followed by measurement.\n\n**State-vector simulator:** A simulator that stores the amplitudes of a pure quantum state.\n\n**Transpilation:** Hardware-aware translation and optimization of a circuit.\n\n---\n\n# References\n\n[1]: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/learning/en/courses/basics-of-quantum-information \"IBM Quantum Learning: Basics of Quantum Information\"\n\n[2]: https://qiskit.qotlabs.org/learning/courses/quantum-computing-in-practice \"IBM Quantum Learning: Quantum Computing in Practice\"\n\n[3]: https://doi.org/10.1103/RevModPhys.68.1049 \"Quantum Computation and Quantum Information by Michael A. Nielsen and Isaac L. Chuang\"\n\n[4]: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1666-5 \"Quantum supremacy using a programmable superconducting processor\"\n\n[5]: https://theory.caltech.edu/~preskill/ph229/ \"John Preskill: Lecture Notes for Physics 229, Quantum Information and Computation\"\n\n[6]: https://www.nist.gov/quantum-information-science \"National Institute of Standards and Technology: Quantum Information Science\"\n\n## Key formulas: derivation and interpretation\n\n### Classical state-vector simulation cost\n\nAn n-qubit pure state is represented by \\(2^n\\) complex amplitudes:\n\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_{x=0}^{2^n-1}\\alpha_x|x\\rangle.\n\\]\n\n**Derivation:** Each of the n qubits has two basis choices. The product rule gives \\(2^n\\) possible bit strings, and one amplitude \\(\\alpha_x\\) is required for each basis state \\(|x\\rangle\\). This exponential growth is why classical simulation becomes difficult as n increases.\n\n### State normalization\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{x=0}^{2^n-1}|\\alpha_x|^2=1.\n\\]\n\nEach term is the probability of observing basis string \\(x\\). The sum equals one because the basis strings exhaust all possible computational-basis outcomes.\n\n### NISQ interpretation\n\nNISQ devices operate with a finite number of imperfect physical qubits. Their useful circuit depth is limited by gate errors, readout errors, and decoherence. Therefore, a simulator can be ideal and noiseless, while a hardware experiment may require noise models, calibration, error mitigation, and repeated shots.\n\n### How the Chapter 11 formulas are obtained\n\n#### Deriving state-vector size\n\n1. Each qubit can contribute a 0 or 1 to a basis string.\n2. For n independent positions, the multiplication rule gives 2^n strings.\n3. A general state needs one amplitude for each string, so it contains 2^n complex amplitudes.\n4. This is an exponential memory requirement for a classical statevector simulator.\n\n#### Deriving normalization\n\n1. Write the state as \\(|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_x\\alpha_x|x\\rangle\\).\n2. The computational basis states are orthonormal: \\(\\langle x|y\\rangle=\\delta_{xy}\\).\n3. Compute the norm: \\(\\langle\\psi|\\psi\\rangle=\\sum_x|\\alpha_x|^2\\).\n4. Set the norm to 1 for a valid quantum state.\n\n**Variables:** n is qubit count; x labels a basis bit string; \\(\\alpha_x\\) is its amplitude; \\(2^n\\) is the statevector length; \\(\\delta_{xy}\\) is the Kronecker delta.\n\n## Chapter assessment\n\n### One-mark questions and answers\n\n1. **Question:** What is a quantum simulator?\n   **Answer:** Software or hardware that models quantum circuits or systems.\n\n2. **Question:** What does NISQ mean?\n   **Answer:** Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum.\n\n3. **Question:** What is gate-based hardware?\n   **Answer:** Hardware that computes using sequences of quantum gates.\n\n4. **Question:** Why are simulators useful?\n   **Answer:** They support learning, testing, and debugging circuits.\n\n5. **Question:** Name a major hardware challenge.\n   **Answer:** Noise and decoherence.\n\n### Chapter quiz\n\n1. What is a quantum simulator?\n\n2. What does NISQ mean?\n\n3. What is gate-based hardware?\n\n4. Why are simulators useful?\n\n5. Name a major hardware challenge.\n\n### Quiz answer key\n\n1. **Software or hardware that models quantum circuits or systems.**\n\n2. **Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum.**\n\n3. **Hardware that computes using sequences of quantum gates.**\n\n4. **They support learning, testing, and debugging circuits.**\n\n5. **Noise and decoherence.**\n\n---\n"
} as const;

